262
00:11:13.983 --> 00:11:14.940
It's like super harsh.

263
00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:16.612
It's brutal, it's brutal.

264
00:11:16.612 --> 00:11:19.050
Laz, he is hilarious.

265
00:11:19.050 --> 00:11:23.290
Well, Laz always has some element to mess with runners.

266
00:11:23.290 --> 00:11:26.740
And so technically everyone that doesn't finish

267
00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:29.350
as a final runner, is a DNF.

268
00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:31.950
So even if he finished the loop

269
00:11:31.950 --> 00:11:32.990
and didn't start another one,

270
00:11:32.990 --> 00:11:34.947
would he get second or just everybody DNF,

271
00:11:34.947 --> 00:11:36.420
except the person who wins?

272
00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:37.401
Everyone DNF's.

273
00:11:37.401 --> 00:11:38.234
(both laughing)

274
00:11:38.234 --> 00:11:39.717
Except the person who wins.

275
00:11:39.717 --> 00:11:40.732
Brutal stuff, second place.

276
00:11:40.732 --> 00:11:41.565
Yeah, Laz, is brutal.

277
00:11:41.565 --> 00:11:43.980
There's the last person, and that's it.

278
00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:47.680
Exactly, so if you're trying to keep your DNF's down,

279
00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:49.280
it might not be the best moment.

280
00:11:50.644 --> 00:11:51.477
Oh, that's so funny.

281
00:11:51.477 --> 00:11:54.450
Well, I mean Lazarus,

282
00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:57.410
this guy is like an all time world-class character.

283
00:11:57.410 --> 00:12:00.403
He is, he is such a high level of that.

284
00:12:00.403 --> 00:12:04.530
Like his philosophy is so interesting

285
00:12:04.530 --> 00:12:05.830
to even spend time with him,

286
00:12:05.830 --> 00:12:08.960
just because everything he says is so philosophical.

287
00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:11.140
And I love how the race is actually named

288
00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:13.390
after his dog, Big's.

289
00:12:13.390 --> 00:12:15.340
and now he has a little dog, Little,

290
00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:18.030
has a race as well named after him.

291
00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:21.757
And Laz, you think maybe from the outside

292
00:12:24.520 --> 00:12:26.960
that he's extremely masochistic

293
00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:30.540
or maybe he's got some sort of like a vendetta,

294
00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:32.750
to really be cruel to runners,

295
00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:36.070
but he does get some humor out of things.

296
00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:40.520
But he really is interested in seeing...

297
00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:42.560
He loves watching people

298
00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:44.630
work through those extreme challenges

299
00:12:44.630 --> 00:12:46.330
and he gets inspired by that.

300
00:12:46.330 --> 00:12:47.810
Where does that come from?

301
00:12:47.810 --> 00:12:50.510
I mean, he's not a runner himself.

302
00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:52.710
He is actually going back into the day.

303
00:12:52.710 --> 00:12:55.750
So back in the sixties and seventies,

304
00:12:55.750 --> 00:12:58.650
he was a big runner in Tennessee

305
00:12:58.650 --> 00:13:00.410
and it's interesting 'cause his dad

306
00:13:00.410 --> 00:13:02.410
was actually a NASA scientist

307
00:13:02.410 --> 00:13:06.943
and he actually worked on the lunar module

308
00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:10.313
coming off the moon.

309
00:13:11.625 --> 00:13:12.920
So he's a really brilliant guy

310
00:13:12.920 --> 00:13:16.343
and he has a ton of interesting history on his back,

311
00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:22.020
but he's kind of taken that he's very particular

312
00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:24.054
about organization too.

313
00:13:24.054 --> 00:13:25.370
Like if you had talked to his wife, Sandra,

314
00:13:25.370 --> 00:13:26.230
like it's so funny,

315
00:13:26.230 --> 00:13:29.363
he's got a certain way that every flag has to be rolled out.

316
00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:32.560
Everything has to be just perfect.

317
00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:35.170
And I mean, we're not even talking about Barkley yet.

318
00:13:35.170 --> 00:13:36.550
I know we're getting there, yeah.

319
00:13:36.550 --> 00:13:38.860
So he is definitely...

320
00:13:39.778 --> 00:13:44.190
I really do admire him for what he is done with the sport

321
00:13:46.190 --> 00:13:49.500
and he's captivated people in so many ways

322
00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:51.810
that we can't even begin to describe.

323
00:13:51.810 --> 00:13:55.620
Yeah, he's sort of single-handedly created a whole new

324
00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:57.213
genre of running.

325
00:13:58.279 --> 00:14:01.770
The unfinishable Barkley marathons,

326
00:14:01.770 --> 00:14:06.200
and this whole backyard format, which he didn't invent it,

327
00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:08.200
but he made it a thing here,

328
00:14:08.200 --> 00:14:09.660
at least in the United States.

329
00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:11.150
Yeah, he actually invented them.

330
00:14:11.150 --> 00:14:11.983
Oh, he did?

331
00:14:11.983 --> 00:14:13.420
Yeah, he invented that.

332
00:14:13.420 --> 00:14:16.800
So it's now spread to over 62 countries

333
00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:19.440
and they have hundreds of events,

334
00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:23.820
there is over 13,000 people participating already.

335
00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:27.960
And it's really exploded in just the last five years,

336
00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:32.740
last four years, it's just growing by madness.

337
00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:34.650
The format makes it really fun.

338
00:14:34.650 --> 00:14:36.220
There's a gamesmanship to it.

339
00:14:36.220 --> 00:14:39.890
And I suspect from a race Oregon organizer, point of view,

340
00:14:39.890 --> 00:14:41.590
it's a lot easier to conduct,

341
00:14:41.590 --> 00:14:45.730
than a race that's traversing like a huge swath of land

342
00:14:45.730 --> 00:14:47.280
in a point-to-point kind of way.

343
00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:49.180
It's true, yeah.

344
00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:50.860
It surprises me at times.

345
00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:53.450
'Cause I'm like, I'll follow their Facebook Page

346
00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:57.170
and I'll see posts from Pakistan or Morocco.

347
00:14:57.170 --> 00:14:59.180
And I see like all these places

348
00:14:59.180 --> 00:15:01.127
where they're formulating this race

349
00:15:01.127 --> 00:15:03.640
and it's exciting to see that, I love that.

350
00:15:03.640 --> 00:15:06.120
It's also less intimidating,

351
00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:08.230
if you're trying to dip your toe in the Ultra world,

352
00:15:08.230 --> 00:15:10.700
'cause you can just go and run four mile loops

353
00:15:10.700 --> 00:15:11.670
until you're done.

354
00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:13.737
It's not like, "Oh, I have to do a hundred miles

355
00:15:13.737 --> 00:15:14.650
"or anything like that."

356
00:15:14.650 --> 00:15:17.120
You can just show up and do what you can.

357
00:15:17.120 --> 00:15:20.960
So it feels like a little bit more welcoming.

358
00:15:20.960 --> 00:15:24.060
Yeah, it is very much welcoming

359
00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:26.850
and it's nice because especially the first day,

360
00:15:26.850 --> 00:15:28.303
there was a lot of comradery.

361
00:15:29.290 --> 00:15:30.820
So it's just like a big party.

362
00:15:30.820 --> 00:15:34.183
You get to like, hang out, share stories.

363
00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:39.950
It's just like a party in the wilderness

364
00:15:39.950 --> 00:15:42.870
and it is moving around, it's fantastic.

365
00:15:42.870 --> 00:15:44.877
Well, it's a party until it's not.

366
00:15:44.877 --> 00:15:49.877
You ran 354.1667, that's how specific this is, (chuckles)

367
00:15:53.900 --> 00:15:56.410
miles, which is 85.

368
00:15:56.410 --> 00:15:57.970
They call them yards, right?

369
00:15:57.970 --> 00:16:00.670
Right. -
Each, four plus mile loop

370
00:16:00.670 --> 00:16:02.188
in 85 hours.

371
00:16:02.188 --> 00:16:05.080
I can't even wrap my head around that.

372
00:16:05.080 --> 00:16:08.500
And it's so much further beyond anything that you had done

373
00:16:09.970 --> 00:16:12.960
in that format or even outside of that format.

374
00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:17.280
Like it's a whole new threshold of experience for you.

375
00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:18.318
Yeah, it's wild.

376
00:16:18.318 --> 00:16:23.318
During the race, I actually went through a low part

377
00:16:23.560 --> 00:16:24.920
around like mile,

378
00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:28.120
I think about 140 or 170, rather.

379
00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:33.120
And it's incredible because I had a battle inside my mind,

380
00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:39.240
like the side that was not wanting to submit at all,

381
00:16:39.240 --> 00:16:41.220
but I had like a little bit creeping in,

382
00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:44.317
I thought of like, "Well, it would be nice to just go relax

383
00:16:44.317 --> 00:16:46.697
"in a hotel, and they'll put my feet up."

384
00:16:46.697 --> 00:16:49.977
And like, "How long is this thing gonna go for?"

385
00:16:49.977 --> 00:16:51.160
And you're only halfway in.

386
00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:55.510
And you don't know how long it's gonna go.

387
00:16:55.510 --> 00:16:57.350
I had no idea at that time.

388
00:16:57.350 --> 00:17:00.620
And so to get through that dark place,

389
00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:02.300
I like to share with my crew chief.

390
00:17:02.300 --> 00:17:03.770
And having a strong crew chief,

391
00:17:03.770 --> 00:17:05.930
is really important in this race.

392
00:17:05.930 --> 00:17:08.350
And when I got back to the tent,

393
00:17:08.350 --> 00:17:10.450
I made sure no one else was nearby.

394
00:17:10.450 --> 00:17:12.237
And I just said real quietly at Judd, I said,

395
00:17:12.237 --> 00:17:15.400
"Hey, I'm struggling through this a bit right now."

396
00:17:15.400 --> 00:17:17.840
And he came up with a good idea.

397
00:17:17.840 --> 00:17:20.197
He's like, "Just visualize that you're running back

398
00:17:20.197 --> 00:17:21.733
"and forth to work."

399
00:17:21.733 --> 00:17:22.790
'Cause I run, commute to work.

400
00:17:22.790 --> 00:17:25.450
And so that was incredible,

401
00:17:25.450 --> 00:17:29.440
because at that point I was really able to just go beyond it

402
00:17:29.440 --> 00:17:31.530
and then just focus on each loop

403
00:17:31.530 --> 00:17:33.537
and just imagine I was running back and forth to work.

404
00:17:33.537 --> 00:17:35.450
It was so easy at that point.

405
00:17:35.450 --> 00:17:37.640
Which is kind of about the same distance, right?

406
00:17:37.640 --> 00:17:38.473
Three, four miles?
Yeah.

407
00:17:38.473 --> 00:17:39.960
It's about the same distance.

408
00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:43.310
So it's incredible how just changing your mindset

409
00:17:44.500 --> 00:17:45.710
can make such an impact.

410
00:17:45.710 --> 00:17:48.200
And I really didn't go through any other...

411
00:17:48.200 --> 00:17:49.033
I went through...

412
00:17:49.033 --> 00:17:51.600
It was tough in the fourth day,

413
00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:54.318
but I didn't go through a point that was like as mentally,

414
00:17:54.318 --> 00:17:57.940
like where I was in a dark place like that.

415
00:17:57.940 --> 00:18:02.940
And then reframing it through that shift in perspective,

416
00:18:03.500 --> 00:18:07.250
gave you this boost that carried you for another 170?

417
00:18:07.250 --> 00:18:08.083
Yeah.

418
00:18:08.083 --> 00:18:10.810
And I know you had a crazy moment at 300, right?

419
00:18:10.810 --> 00:18:12.200
Right, that's a whole nother-

420
00:18:12.200 --> 00:18:13.200
Yeah, let's talk about that.

421
00:18:13.200 --> 00:18:18.200
TAVI, so yeah, basically once we got into that third night

422
00:18:19.220 --> 00:18:23.580
and it kind of becomes automatic and you're just like...

423
00:18:23.580 --> 00:18:25.870
You get yourself back to the tent,

424
00:18:25.870 --> 00:18:27.320
you're laying down for five minutes,

425
00:18:27.320 --> 00:18:29.117
you're eating as much as humanly possible,

426
00:18:29.117 --> 00:18:31.510
and you're just lining up at the line again,

427
00:18:31.510 --> 00:18:34.573
and you're wondering how long are these people gonna go for?

428
00:18:35.810 --> 00:18:37.970
It's a poker game, you can't play your hand, right?

429
00:18:37.970 --> 00:18:40.780
You can't let anyone know that you're feeling down.

430
00:18:40.780 --> 00:18:41.730
Oh no, no.

431
00:18:41.730 --> 00:18:44.572
That would be destruction.

432
00:18:44.572 --> 00:18:49.000
So then you just keep on playing it out.

433
00:18:49.000 --> 00:18:51.270
And during the third night,

434
00:18:51.270 --> 00:18:53.890
there was something that kind of hit me.

435
00:18:53.890 --> 00:18:57.940
I think I just got very automatic with it

436
00:18:57.940 --> 00:19:01.610
and I felt pretty strong.

437
00:19:01.610 --> 00:19:02.773
Like I was just like dialed in,

438
00:19:02.773 --> 00:19:06.240
then I was back to my game plan,

439
00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:10.040
that, okay, I'm gonna go as long as this thing carries it,

440
00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:12.320
however long that might be.

441
00:19:12.320 --> 00:19:16.630
And first, Roberts at that time

442
00:19:16.630 --> 00:19:20.080
was actually really, really struggling.

443
00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:22.220
The Japanese runner, Mori Mori,

444
00:19:22.220 --> 00:19:23.663
he was looking strong.

445
00:19:24.630 --> 00:19:28.670
But Chris Robertson in the last three loops of the nighttime

446
00:19:28.670 --> 00:19:29.580
on the third night,

447
00:19:29.580 --> 00:19:32.240
he got to the point where he was actually running sideways.

448
00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:35.090
Like he was actually hip scot, like skipping sideways.

449
00:19:35.090 --> 00:19:37.100
And I was like, "Wow".

450
00:19:37.100 --> 00:19:38.310
I was really impressed by that.

451
00:19:38.310 --> 00:19:40.270
I'm like, "And this guy staying in here."

452
00:19:40.270 --> 00:19:43.770
Because I was pretty sure he was gonna like tag out.

453
00:19:43.770 --> 00:19:44.759
He's got like three minutes left on his loop.

454
00:19:44.759 --> 00:19:49.660
And he is so serious to the point where he's willing

455
00:19:49.660 --> 00:19:52.880
to skip sideways to get himself down to the...

456
00:19:52.880 --> 00:19:54.330
Which was not easy to do.

457
00:19:54.330 --> 00:19:58.451
So he pivoted his body and then went-

458
00:19:58.451 --> 00:19:59.580
He pivoted his body because his knee was hurt.

459
00:19:59.580 --> 00:20:01.360
And he literally skipped sideways.

460
00:20:01.360 --> 00:20:04.673
I was like, "You're running sideways after like 200

461
00:20:04.673 --> 00:20:05.597
"and some miles?

462
00:20:05.597 --> 00:20:07.797
"These guys are never gonna give up either."

463
00:20:09.118 --> 00:20:10.100
Yeah, so I saw some lads.

464
00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:13.400
I'm like "Lads, none of the three of us are gonna submit."

465
00:20:13.400 --> 00:20:16.175
I said that I think somewhere after like 300 miles,

466
00:20:16.175 --> 00:20:17.470
I'm like, "It's just not gonna happen."

467
00:20:17.470 --> 00:20:20.450
He's like, "It's going to happen eventually."

468
00:20:20.450 --> 00:20:21.860
One way or another, right?

469
00:20:21.860 --> 00:20:22.693
Right.

470
00:20:22.693 --> 00:20:24.080
So the year prior,

471
00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:27.280
when you were going tad-a-tat with Courtney,

472
00:20:27.280 --> 00:20:31.565
you pooped out at 240 or something like that?

473
00:20:31.565 --> 00:20:36.480
277, yeah, 277, it should get at 281.

474
00:20:36.480 --> 00:20:40.850
When you came up on that kind of mile marker this time,

475
00:20:40.850 --> 00:20:41.770
what do you think...

476
00:20:41.770 --> 00:20:43.970
how are you able to get to a different gear?

477
00:20:43.970 --> 00:20:45.500
Did you do something different in training?

478
00:20:45.500 --> 00:20:48.023
Is it experience, what changed?

479
00:20:49.650 --> 00:20:52.440
Well, I thought back to 2017,

480
00:20:52.440 --> 00:20:55.090
because that's when Jim and I,

481
00:20:55.090 --> 00:20:59.010
the French runner, we both were running.

482
00:20:59.010 --> 00:21:02.510
We ran like an additional day beyond everyone else.

483
00:21:02.510 --> 00:21:07.070
And we ended up going to 241 and I submitted at 241.

484
00:21:07.070 --> 00:21:09.040
That was the cyst that year.

485
00:21:09.040 --> 00:21:10.860
And I thought about last...

486
00:21:10.860 --> 00:21:12.700
Back then he said...

487
00:21:12.700 --> 00:21:13.987
He had this big thing about saying,

488
00:21:13.987 --> 00:21:15.327
"Okay, I think you guys are gonna...

489
00:21:15.327 --> 00:21:17.610
"Someone's gonna make it to 300 miles."

490
00:21:17.610 --> 00:21:20.877
And like that year in 2017, his first year I did it.

491
00:21:20.877 --> 00:21:24.477
And so his idea of 300 kind of got in my head, and I'm like,

492
00:21:24.477 --> 00:21:25.967
"Well, shoot, we're gonna get to 300.

493
00:21:25.967 --> 00:21:27.577
"And neither of us are gonna want to quit either.

494
00:21:27.577 --> 00:21:29.730
"I gotta get back to teaching."

495
00:21:29.730 --> 00:21:33.610
And it just became this number that was like out there.

496
00:21:33.610 --> 00:21:36.050
And then last year with Courtney,

497
00:21:36.050 --> 00:21:37.810
I told last somewhere before,

498
00:21:37.810 --> 00:21:39.830
I think three laps before I stopped,

499
00:21:39.830 --> 00:21:41.617
I'm like, "Laz, we've got this,

500
00:21:41.617 --> 00:21:42.857
"we're gonna beat a 300.

501
00:21:42.857 --> 00:21:44.500
"There's no way we're stopping."

502
00:21:44.500 --> 00:21:48.920
And then sure enough, three laps later, my mind wouldn't,

503
00:21:48.920 --> 00:21:50.540
it gave up.

504
00:21:50.540 --> 00:21:53.520
So I felt at this race,

505
00:21:53.520 --> 00:21:55.980
I really didn't wanna give up mentally.

506
00:21:55.980 --> 00:21:59.040
Like if I was gonna be out,

507
00:21:59.040 --> 00:22:02.000
it was gonna be based on me not making it back in time.

508
00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:05.800
Every other time I did this when I submitted,

509
00:22:05.800 --> 00:22:08.520
it was because my mind had given up,

510
00:22:08.520 --> 00:22:10.910
it wasn't because I timed out.

511
00:22:10.910 --> 00:22:12.483
And so this race I won,

512
00:22:14.037 --> 00:22:15.330
if I was going to go out,

513
00:22:15.330 --> 00:22:17.480
it was gonna be based on not making it back

514
00:22:17.480 --> 00:22:18.820
in the 60 minutes.

515
00:22:18.820 --> 00:22:23.820
Not based on like, "Oh, I don't think I can go any further."

516
00:22:23.900 --> 00:22:26.390
'Cause last year with Courtney,

517
00:22:26.390 --> 00:22:28.920
I've thought it was really amazing running

518
00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:30.100
with her, number one.

519
00:22:30.100 --> 00:22:32.640
Like we were in a dynamic

520
00:22:32.640 --> 00:22:34.180
where we were supportive of each other,

521
00:22:34.180 --> 00:22:36.228
which was really special.

522
00:22:36.228 --> 00:22:40.200
We both have a history with the 24 Hour USA Team,

523
00:22:40.200 --> 00:22:41.850
but like to work with somebody,

524
00:22:41.850 --> 00:22:44.900
it makes it just an amazing experience

525
00:22:44.900 --> 00:22:47.173
where you're working for something greater.

526
00:22:48.775 --> 00:22:52.000
And so last year I just got to this place

527
00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:55.293
where I was in my mind.

528
00:22:56.380 --> 00:22:58.740
I thought I was losing time on the laps

529
00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:00.970
and I didn't think I could make it many more laps.

530
00:23:00.970 --> 00:23:02.950
And so I was like, "Okay."

531
00:23:02.950 --> 00:23:05.270
I started like hallucinate a bit more.

532
00:23:05.270 --> 00:23:08.050
And I said, "Okay, I think this is it."

533
00:23:08.050 --> 00:23:10.340
And so I gave up.

534
00:23:10.340 --> 00:23:15.300
So this year I've really trained my mind more to not submit.

535
00:23:15.300 --> 00:23:17.200
Like if I go out,

536
00:23:17.200 --> 00:23:18.047
it's gonna be physical, not-

537
00:23:18.047 --> 00:23:20.304
And what does that training look like?

538
00:23:20.304 --> 00:23:25.120
Is there a specific program or method to this madness?

539
00:23:25.120 --> 00:23:26.780
The methods to the madness.

540
00:23:26.780 --> 00:23:30.560
Well, part helped the identity Backyard Ultra in March,

541
00:23:30.560 --> 00:23:33.630
where actually was the final person standing.

542
00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:35.920
And that was the first time I did that.

543
00:23:35.920 --> 00:23:38.690
And so we did that in Ohio Backyard Ultra,

544
00:23:38.690 --> 00:23:41.350
it went to about 240 so miles.

545
00:23:41.350 --> 00:23:44.260
So that helped to have that victory on my back,

546
00:23:44.260 --> 00:23:47.460
to understand what it's like to be the final person,

547
00:23:47.460 --> 00:23:51.080
and then leading up to this year, I did two races.

548
00:23:51.080 --> 00:23:54.150
I did Badwater, which helped-

549
00:23:54.150 --> 00:23:54.983
Which you won.

550
00:23:54.983 --> 00:23:59.260
Little Badwater, and then I did the FANS 24 Hour race,

551
00:23:59.260 --> 00:24:00.220
seven weeks out.

552
00:24:00.220 --> 00:24:04.860
And I did the Superior 100 race, five weeks out.

553
00:24:04.860 --> 00:24:06.630
So doing a road race,

554
00:24:06.630 --> 00:24:11.090
and then a trail race with 20,000 feet, five weeks out,

555
00:24:11.090 --> 00:24:14.443
it helped, including both dynamics,

556
00:24:15.640 --> 00:24:18.513
but also conversation with my crew chief.

557
00:24:19.800 --> 00:24:23.170
We had it dialed down in terms of what we wanted to do.

558
00:24:23.170 --> 00:24:24.010
I think that helps a lot.

559
00:24:24.010 --> 00:24:28.880
What are the characteristics or qualities

560
00:24:28.880 --> 00:24:30.170
that you look for?

561
00:24:30.170 --> 00:24:32.860
Like what makes a great crew chief, a great crew chief?

562
00:24:32.860 --> 00:24:37.860
'Cause I think people really underestimate how crucial,

563
00:24:38.130 --> 00:24:41.010
those people are, who are supporting you.

564
00:24:41.010 --> 00:24:43.640
And you know, many of us have had experiences.

565
00:24:43.640 --> 00:24:47.230
Like we get our friends to do it, or have my wife do it ,

566
00:24:47.230 --> 00:24:50.140
and my stepsons and it's great,

567
00:24:50.140 --> 00:24:53.270
but they're not like dialed in as crew people.

568
00:24:53.270 --> 00:24:56.010
And when shit's gone off the rails,

569
00:24:56.010 --> 00:24:58.410
like you want someone there who really understands

570
00:24:58.410 --> 00:24:59.840
what you're going through and can make sure

571
00:24:59.840 --> 00:25:02.320
that you're getting what you need.

572
00:25:02.320 --> 00:25:04.390
Yeah, I think it's so important.

573
00:25:04.390 --> 00:25:09.390
With the crew, number one is a positive mindset.

574
00:25:09.470 --> 00:25:12.000
Like no matter what happens,

575
00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:15.280
they are going to have a mindset that's positive.

576
00:25:15.280 --> 00:25:17.913
So there could be crazy, crazy things happening,

577
00:25:18.790 --> 00:25:21.900
anything you could imagine and no matter what that is,

578
00:25:21.900 --> 00:25:23.912
their mind is gonna be positive

579
00:25:23.912 --> 00:25:26.050
and they're gonna be looking through problem solving.

580
00:25:26.050 --> 00:25:28.240
So problem solving is great.

581
00:25:28.240 --> 00:25:32.620
Having someone that is loyal and has your...

582
00:25:32.620 --> 00:25:35.600
That's huge, that you feel comfortable with.

583
00:25:35.600 --> 00:25:39.360
So it's like they don't have to be an amazing runner at all,

584
00:25:39.360 --> 00:25:40.970
at all, not, not at all.

585
00:25:40.970 --> 00:25:45.440
It's more important that they are flexible.

586
00:25:45.440 --> 00:25:47.340
They're willing to...

587
00:25:47.340 --> 00:25:48.200
They're excited about...

588
00:25:48.200 --> 00:25:49.920
It's also important to have someone who's excited.

589
00:25:49.920 --> 00:25:51.707
You don't wanna have someone come out there who's like,

590
00:25:51.707 --> 00:25:52.883
"Oh my God, I will be out here for like..."

591
00:25:52.883 --> 00:25:54.346
Can you imagine?

592
00:25:54.346 --> 00:25:55.446
And that's a long time.

593
00:25:55.446 --> 00:25:56.920
Well, there's-
135 miles is a long time.

594
00:25:56.920 --> 00:25:59.450
There's legendary stories about people bailing

595
00:25:59.450 --> 00:26:01.900
in the middle of ram and all that kind of stuff.

596
00:26:01.900 --> 00:26:04.520
Like, "Wow, this is way more work than I thought."

597
00:26:04.520 --> 00:26:06.627
And the crews fighting and they're not getting along.

598
00:26:06.627 --> 00:26:09.010
And the whole thing just craters.

599
00:26:09.010 --> 00:26:09.860
It's important.

600
00:26:10.863 --> 00:26:15.470
I tried to build the crew months and months

601
00:26:15.470 --> 00:26:16.660
before the actual event.

602
00:26:16.660 --> 00:26:18.760
And I don't have crew for all my races,

603
00:26:18.760 --> 00:26:21.660
but like on my A races,

604
00:26:21.660 --> 00:26:25.550
I definitely try to build that prior to going.

605
00:26:25.550 --> 00:26:27.240
So like Badwater, for example,

606
00:26:27.240 --> 00:26:30.220
is a fundraise for the crew component,

607
00:26:30.220 --> 00:26:31.680
'cause you have the crew

608
00:26:32.612 --> 00:26:36.370
that is going to basically follow you the whole 135 miles.

609
00:26:36.370 --> 00:26:38.800
And it's so important to have a group

610
00:26:38.800 --> 00:26:40.173
that gets along together,

611
00:26:41.210 --> 00:26:45.790
but also people that bring different dynamics to the team.

612
00:26:45.790 --> 00:26:49.180
So someone might be the person that does a lot of miles,

613
00:26:49.180 --> 00:26:51.920
but you need someone who's extremely good at like logistics

614
00:26:51.920 --> 00:26:54.520
and organizing, that's really important.

615
00:26:54.520 --> 00:26:57.220
And, you think outside the box.

616
00:26:57.220 --> 00:27:00.340
The crew is really important

617
00:27:00.340 --> 00:27:02.490
in a lot of these Ultras, for sure.

618
00:27:02.490 --> 00:27:04.890
They gotta be five steps ahead at all times.

619
00:27:04.890 --> 00:27:07.130
Do you for example, with Badwater,

620
00:27:07.130 --> 00:27:10.280
do you like it when someone's running with you all the time

621
00:27:10.280 --> 00:27:12.080
or do you like them to kind of bump and run

622
00:27:12.080 --> 00:27:14.260
or what's your kind of mode?

623
00:27:14.260 --> 00:27:17.320
So for Badwater I definitely like having someone

624
00:27:17.320 --> 00:27:20.160
run with me, it's like after mile 42 now,

625
00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:21.930
you could have someone run with you all the way

626
00:27:21.930 --> 00:27:22.957
to the finish.

627
00:27:22.957 --> 00:27:26.960
And I like rotating it because after a while,

628
00:27:26.960 --> 00:27:28.270
I always want someone fresh.

629
00:27:28.270 --> 00:27:31.380
So I kind of feel like I take on their energy force.

630
00:27:31.380 --> 00:27:32.880
So I get someone new in,

631
00:27:32.880 --> 00:27:34.670
it's like I kind of feel their energy,

632
00:27:34.670 --> 00:27:36.110
even though I'm running in front of them

633
00:27:36.110 --> 00:27:38.840
and it's not like pacing in the traditional sense

634
00:27:38.840 --> 00:27:40.320
or running behind you.

635
00:27:40.320 --> 00:27:42.220
It's like, I feel their energy force.

636
00:27:42.220 --> 00:27:45.500
And it's like, it helps me to go faster.

637
00:27:45.500 --> 00:27:48.110
So I like having a rotation,

638
00:27:48.110 --> 00:27:51.333
I love having someone with me in Badwater.

639
00:27:52.640 --> 00:27:54.150
Sometimes we're not even speaking,

640
00:27:54.150 --> 00:27:58.770
it's just being out there and running like a team,

641
00:27:58.770 --> 00:28:02.770
it's like, we're wild animals migrating across the plains.

642
00:28:02.770 --> 00:28:06.540
And that's huge, is a huge impact.

643
00:28:06.540 --> 00:28:10.130
So you're at mile 300 of Big's.

644
00:28:10.130 --> 00:28:12.910
Like I gotta hear about this like hallucinatory

645
00:28:12.910 --> 00:28:14.930
nirvana moment that you had.

646
00:28:14.930 --> 00:28:19.930
Yeah, so this year I would just say like,

647
00:28:20.620 --> 00:28:21.990
it was kinda crazy,

648
00:28:21.990 --> 00:28:26.140
because you go through these lows and highs,

649
00:28:26.140 --> 00:28:27.770
but going into 300,

650
00:28:27.770 --> 00:28:30.000
it just felt like we were invincible

651
00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:34.670
almost like in Mori Mori, the Japanese runner,

652
00:28:34.670 --> 00:28:37.290
he just was fired up.

653
00:28:37.290 --> 00:28:38.380
We were both fired up.

654
00:28:38.380 --> 00:28:40.010
So once we got past 300,

655
00:28:40.010 --> 00:28:42.020
we start doing crazy things.

656
00:28:42.020 --> 00:28:46.250
And like some of it was like strategic on my part,

657
00:28:46.250 --> 00:28:49.033
trying to like wear him down a little bit.

658
00:28:50.060 --> 00:28:53.090
But some of it was just like in passion,

659
00:28:53.090 --> 00:28:56.800
like just feeling like fired up and like, let's just go.

660
00:28:56.800 --> 00:28:59.720
So we started doing things that you probably shouldn't do

661
00:28:59.720 --> 00:29:00.820
after 300 miles.

662
00:29:00.820 --> 00:29:02.790
Like we start running up the hill,

663
00:29:02.790 --> 00:29:05.104
we've been walking up since day one.

664
00:29:05.104 --> 00:29:07.640
And then we start sprinting through the camp.

665
00:29:07.640 --> 00:29:09.250
Again, everyone fired up,

666
00:29:09.250 --> 00:29:11.470
like just kind of ridiculous things.

667
00:29:11.470 --> 00:29:14.530
And sometimes we just like start running super faster

668
00:29:14.530 --> 00:29:16.410
to woods and places we probably shouldn't,

669
00:29:16.410 --> 00:29:17.560
where you'd like crush.

670
00:29:21.370 --> 00:29:22.640
Sometimes we'd be hollering.

671
00:29:22.640 --> 00:29:24.410
Like, we'd just start yelling.

672
00:29:24.410 --> 00:29:26.800
Not in like aggressive way towards each other,

673
00:29:26.800 --> 00:29:30.220
but just because we were fired up and excited about it.

674
00:29:30.220 --> 00:29:35.220
So it was wild to hit that mark

675
00:29:35.440 --> 00:29:38.240
and then feel all kinds of new energy.

676
00:29:38.240 --> 00:29:41.420
I felt in some times more energy than I felt the whole race

677
00:29:41.420 --> 00:29:43.260
at like loop two, where I felt more energy.

678
00:29:43.260 --> 00:29:45.411
Did you go onto some crazy gear

679
00:29:45.411 --> 00:29:47.286
that you didn't even know that you had?

680
00:29:47.286 --> 00:29:48.119
Don't even know that you had,

681
00:29:48.119 --> 00:29:50.120
like, that was what was so special about it.

682
00:29:50.120 --> 00:29:53.300
'Cause you get beyond 300 miles and then you realize,

683
00:29:53.300 --> 00:29:54.620
wow, there's these like...

684
00:29:54.620 --> 00:29:57.760
You have more force than you ever had even in the first day.

685
00:29:57.760 --> 00:29:59.030
You're like, "Where'd this come from?"

686
00:29:59.030 --> 00:30:00.264
Yeah, like where does it come from?

687
00:30:00.264 --> 00:30:02.308
What do you make of that?

688
00:30:02.308 --> 00:30:03.540
It was like-
For somebody who's pushing

689
00:30:03.540 --> 00:30:05.590
the envelope to such an extreme

690
00:30:05.590 --> 00:30:07.840
and has been doing this for so long,

691
00:30:07.840 --> 00:30:10.190
you'd think you kind of know at this point,

692
00:30:10.190 --> 00:30:13.970
like where that limit sits and yet you have this huge...

693
00:30:13.970 --> 00:30:16.300
I mean, it wasn't like you went four miles longer

694
00:30:16.300 --> 00:30:17.290
than you did last year.

695
00:30:17.290 --> 00:30:21.840
Like you just exploded the ceiling on probably what you...

696
00:30:21.840 --> 00:30:23.040
300 being the mark,

697
00:30:23.040 --> 00:30:25.217
and then you go so much further past that.

698
00:30:25.217 --> 00:30:28.550
And I really didn't think Chris was gonna even submit it.

699
00:30:28.550 --> 00:30:32.100
Like when he did at 354 or 350,

700
00:30:32.100 --> 00:30:34.390
I sure thought we'd definitely going through

701
00:30:34.390 --> 00:30:35.730
the whole night here.

702
00:30:35.730 --> 00:30:37.580
'Cause it's just pretty much,

703
00:30:37.580 --> 00:30:39.010
once you hit the road is easier.

704
00:30:39.010 --> 00:30:42.320
So as long as you can overcome the sleep deprivation,

705
00:30:42.320 --> 00:30:44.210
you don't have like a serious injury.

706
00:30:44.210 --> 00:30:47.913
You can usually just make that out and back in the time.

707
00:30:49.130 --> 00:30:51.780
And so we were making it with plenty of time.

708
00:30:51.780 --> 00:30:53.300
If it weren't for his knee injury,

709
00:30:53.300 --> 00:30:55.840
I think we were gone a lot further too.

710
00:30:55.840 --> 00:30:58.470
And once he submitted,

711
00:30:58.470 --> 00:31:01.510
are you allowed to keep going or that's it?

712
00:31:01.510 --> 00:31:04.080
No, it's like guessing the ethics of a race.

713
00:31:04.080 --> 00:31:05.320
so you only go one more loop.

714
00:31:05.320 --> 00:31:06.550
Right, so even if you...

715
00:31:06.550 --> 00:31:08.470
Do you feel like you could have kept going?

716
00:31:08.470 --> 00:31:09.730
Yeah, absolutely.

717
00:31:09.730 --> 00:31:13.405
I was definitely dialed in for the remainder of that night

718
00:31:13.405 --> 00:31:15.050
and that it would've been challenging going back

719
00:31:15.050 --> 00:31:17.330
on the loop course and the fifth day

720
00:31:17.330 --> 00:31:18.960
would have been interesting,

721
00:31:18.960 --> 00:31:21.220
'cause I don't know how I would have adapted.

722
00:31:21.220 --> 00:31:24.510
But then again, you never know and nothing's guaranteed.

723
00:31:24.510 --> 00:31:26.623
So I mean, something can change.

724
00:31:27.630 --> 00:31:28.727
It can change fast.

725
00:31:28.727 --> 00:31:31.650
And so you always trying to just focus on that loop

726
00:31:31.650 --> 00:31:32.730
in front of you.

727
00:31:32.730 --> 00:31:34.143
Just one loop at a time.

728
00:31:35.012 --> 00:31:36.700
If people are watching on YouTube,

729
00:31:36.700 --> 00:31:38.767
they see the brace on your right hand,

730
00:31:38.767 --> 00:31:40.300
you took the spill,

731
00:31:40.300 --> 00:31:41.343
fractured your hand, but you couldn't vent

732
00:31:41.343 --> 00:31:46.050
any outward facing pain.

733
00:31:46.050 --> 00:31:47.344
You did not...

734
00:31:47.344 --> 00:31:49.200
No, no, no, no, no way, no way.

735
00:31:49.200 --> 00:31:50.319
Not until the finish.

736
00:31:50.319 --> 00:31:52.157
I shook Chris' hand and I was like,

737
00:31:52.157 --> 00:31:53.387
"Oh, that's not good."

738
00:31:57.996 --> 00:32:00.579
(upbeat music)

739
00:32:31.400 --> 00:32:33.853
Yeah, one thing I do actually in races,

740
00:32:34.892 --> 00:32:35.940
I don't know how unique it is,

741
00:32:35.940 --> 00:32:38.120
but I always like in my A races,

742
00:32:38.120 --> 00:32:40.370
I always take on like a spirit animal.

743
00:32:40.370 --> 00:32:42.370
And so for this race,

744
00:32:42.370 --> 00:32:44.630
my spirit animal was a Honey badger.

745
00:32:44.630 --> 00:32:48.337
And so I watched that video, it has 4 million views.

746
00:32:48.337 --> 00:32:50.040
And I was serious about while I was going into the race,

747
00:32:50.040 --> 00:32:51.910
I was like, I'm a Honey badger.

748
00:32:51.910 --> 00:32:54.750
So it doesn't matter what happens, I'm gonna still run.

749
00:32:54.750 --> 00:32:58.930
So, on the fourth day a Honey badger fall down on the rocks,

750
00:32:58.930 --> 00:33:01.470
it doesn't matter, you just get up, keep on running.

751
00:33:01.470 --> 00:33:03.320
At that point of fourth day,

752
00:33:03.320 --> 00:33:06.700
I sure got to a place where it was tough.

753
00:33:06.700 --> 00:33:07.920
Like the fourth day is hard.

754
00:33:07.920 --> 00:33:11.520
Laz didn't really expect people to do that.

755
00:33:11.520 --> 00:33:16.520
And it warms up.

756
00:33:17.700 --> 00:33:20.880
It's not like really hot, but even like 70 degrees

757
00:33:20.880 --> 00:33:23.870
after you've been running for three plus days,

758
00:33:23.870 --> 00:33:25.800
it just takes more energy.

759
00:33:25.800 --> 00:33:28.600
And yeah, I got a little bit dehydrated

760
00:33:28.600 --> 00:33:30.780
doing the running with Mori Mori.

761
00:33:30.780 --> 00:33:32.430
Just doing that crazy running

762
00:33:32.430 --> 00:33:34.230
probably didn't help things either.

763
00:33:34.230 --> 00:33:35.697
Chris Roberts was sitting back thinking,

764
00:33:35.697 --> 00:33:37.587
"Man, you guys are gonna kill each other."

765
00:33:37.587 --> 00:33:39.127
And like, "I'm fine with this."

766
00:33:39.127 --> 00:33:40.950
But Mori passed out.

767
00:33:40.950 --> 00:33:42.070
He went unconscious.

768
00:33:42.070 --> 00:33:44.920
Unfortunately, yeah, he actually literally fell

769
00:33:46.680 --> 00:33:50.827
close to, I think it was maybe 346 miles.

770
00:33:52.590 --> 00:33:55.180
He literally fell and knocked himself out,

771
00:33:55.180 --> 00:33:58.010
for like 20 seconds or something.

772
00:33:58.010 --> 00:34:01.363
But it was enough to just put him over the edge.

773
00:34:02.294 --> 00:34:03.127
He didn't make it back.

774
00:34:03.127 --> 00:34:05.303
He missed the cutoff by maybe a minute or two.

775
00:34:06.207 --> 00:34:11.100
So yeah, it's kind of a crazy race.

776
00:34:11.100 --> 00:34:13.350
It definitely is a crazy race,

777
00:34:13.350 --> 00:34:15.590
but it's exciting,

778
00:34:15.590 --> 00:34:19.950
because of just all the different aspects of it.

779
00:34:19.950 --> 00:34:22.260
There's a lot of psychology in the race

780
00:34:22.260 --> 00:34:24.850
as well as the physiology.

781
00:34:24.850 --> 00:34:28.170
And yeah, it was surprising.

782
00:34:28.170 --> 00:34:30.640
Honestly, I didn't feel anything in my hand.

783
00:34:30.640 --> 00:34:31.670
I knew I did it,

784
00:34:31.670 --> 00:34:33.410
'cause when I fell, I had no energy.

785
00:34:33.410 --> 00:34:34.590
Usually I fall,

786
00:34:34.590 --> 00:34:36.586
The first day I felt like three times.

787
00:34:36.586 --> 00:34:37.970
I don't think I fell the second day at all,

788
00:34:37.970 --> 00:34:38.830
but literally I fell enough.

789
00:34:38.830 --> 00:34:39.810
It'd be easy spots.

790
00:34:39.810 --> 00:34:41.450
Like where I was like just looking off

791
00:34:41.450 --> 00:34:42.800
or talking to someone.

792
00:34:42.800 --> 00:34:43.633
But when I fell,

793
00:34:43.633 --> 00:34:46.560
I had zero energy to catch myself or to roll.

794
00:34:46.560 --> 00:34:49.380
And I just came right down on the hand.

795
00:34:49.380 --> 00:34:52.310
I knew right away I did something to it, but I was like,

796
00:34:52.310 --> 00:34:55.150
well, it's not like essential.

797
00:34:55.150 --> 00:34:56.730
We're gonna deal with that later.

798
00:34:56.730 --> 00:34:58.950
I checked my knee, knee was fine, no problem.

799
00:34:58.950 --> 00:35:00.940
Just keep on going.

800
00:35:00.940 --> 00:35:03.290
It's sort of like that Roger Banister thing

801
00:35:03.290 --> 00:35:05.150
of breaking the four-minute mile.

802
00:35:05.150 --> 00:35:06.990
Like once he does it,

803
00:35:06.990 --> 00:35:09.110
then all kinds of people do it.

804
00:35:09.110 --> 00:35:12.080
People have struggled to eclipse the 300 mile mark.

805
00:35:12.080 --> 00:35:14.410
There is that, I mean you broke the world record

806
00:35:14.410 --> 00:35:17.090
that was set on an easier course in the UK.

807
00:35:17.090 --> 00:35:20.090
That was 300, I don't know, 20 or something like that?

808
00:35:20.090 --> 00:35:22.210
Yeah, John Stalker, he set that in the UK.

809
00:35:22.210 --> 00:35:25.320
So it's not like no one had ever gone over 300 before,

810
00:35:25.320 --> 00:35:27.440
but here you have three guys

811
00:35:27.440 --> 00:35:29.360
who are all competing against each other,

812
00:35:29.360 --> 00:35:30.730
but also supporting each other

813
00:35:30.730 --> 00:35:32.120
to kind of break that ceiling.

814
00:35:32.120 --> 00:35:34.420
And it'll be interesting to see what happens next year.

815
00:35:34.420 --> 00:35:36.280
Like, oh, that's possible.

816
00:35:36.280 --> 00:35:38.690
Like, okay, are we going for 400?

817
00:35:38.690 --> 00:35:40.140
Is that what's happening now?

818
00:35:41.210 --> 00:35:42.043
Who knows?

819
00:35:42.043 --> 00:35:42.876
It's absolutely true.

820
00:35:42.876 --> 00:35:45.500
I don't think the record's gonna stay there that long.

821
00:35:46.391 --> 00:35:48.550
The rate, what is special about it,

822
00:35:48.550 --> 00:35:51.590
is that it did happen on Lazarus' backyard,

823
00:35:51.590 --> 00:35:54.460
and his event, it is tougher.

824
00:35:54.460 --> 00:35:57.350
It's got more technical elements to the course.

825
00:35:57.350 --> 00:36:00.180
There's more like elevation climb,

826
00:36:00.180 --> 00:36:02.030
but there's a lot of variety.

827
00:36:02.030 --> 00:36:04.770
They've now done these backyards in like Mexico

828
00:36:04.770 --> 00:36:08.347
where they have like a thousand feet per lap.

829
00:36:08.347 --> 00:36:09.313
So it's like nuts.

830
00:36:10.370 --> 00:36:11.480
There's a lot of variety,

831
00:36:11.480 --> 00:36:14.820
but this was a tougher course to set that record on.

832
00:36:14.820 --> 00:36:15.990
But I think you're right.

833
00:36:15.990 --> 00:36:17.050
It's gonna be crazy.

834
00:36:17.050 --> 00:36:18.740
Like where is it gonna go?

835
00:36:18.740 --> 00:36:20.250
Have they done one on a track

836
00:36:20.250 --> 00:36:23.580
just to see how far you can do?

837
00:36:23.580 --> 00:36:27.570
Like created the optimal conditions for like a crazy record?

838
00:36:27.570 --> 00:36:31.270
I don't know about track, it's on the spirit of it.

839
00:36:31.270 --> 00:36:35.180
It'd be better to have it like in incorporate trails,

840
00:36:35.180 --> 00:36:36.620
as well as the road.

841
00:36:36.620 --> 00:36:40.100
Mixing the flip-flop between trail and road.

842
00:36:40.100 --> 00:36:43.520
Did you find that helpful because you got a little variety?

843
00:36:43.520 --> 00:36:45.300
Mentally, I would think it would be good.

844
00:36:45.300 --> 00:36:50.000
Yeah, it's wild because you get to a place

845
00:36:50.000 --> 00:36:52.267
where you get tired of one and you're like,

846
00:36:52.267 --> 00:36:54.900
"Oh God, I can't wait to get back to the road.".

847
00:36:54.900 --> 00:36:56.690
Yeah, so when you got like two or three loops to go,

848
00:36:56.690 --> 00:36:58.387
you're like, "Oh my God, it's two more loops

849
00:36:58.387 --> 00:36:59.507
"and I'll be back on the road.

850
00:36:59.507 --> 00:37:01.290
"I can survive forever."

851
00:37:01.290 --> 00:37:02.730
And then when you get back on the road

852
00:37:02.730 --> 00:37:05.890
and you're like out there for like nine hours in the dark

853
00:37:05.890 --> 00:37:07.760
and we had an amazing full moon.

854
00:37:07.760 --> 00:37:10.140
There was coyotes like howling.

855
00:37:10.140 --> 00:37:13.410
It was really special like nighttime,

856
00:37:13.410 --> 00:37:16.750
but nevertheless after nine or 10 hours

857
00:37:16.750 --> 00:37:18.560
of you're out there by yourself,

858
00:37:18.560 --> 00:37:21.450
'cause I wasn't running with Mori Mori or Chris,

859
00:37:21.450 --> 00:37:22.610
we're just out and back.

860
00:37:22.610 --> 00:37:25.240
So you have a lot of time to yourself

861
00:37:25.240 --> 00:37:28.170
to have thoughts within yourself.

862
00:37:28.170 --> 00:37:31.420
And definitely, you're so thankful

863
00:37:31.420 --> 00:37:33.380
when you get back to the trail.

864
00:37:33.380 --> 00:37:34.340
You're like, "Oh, thank God."

865
00:37:34.340 --> 00:37:37.060
Did you put anything in the ears?

866
00:37:37.060 --> 00:37:39.170
Or you have to go...

867
00:37:39.170 --> 00:37:40.070
Do you listen to stuff?

868
00:37:40.070 --> 00:37:43.900
Yeah, you can listen to music on the road portion.

869
00:37:43.900 --> 00:37:46.650
And I find that very helpful.

870
00:37:46.650 --> 00:37:49.220
Laz doesn't allow it on the trail,

871
00:37:49.220 --> 00:37:51.540
unless it's like the final two runners.

872
00:37:51.540 --> 00:37:54.450
Final two, three runners, then he doesn't really mind,

873
00:37:54.450 --> 00:37:56.090
but he doesn't wanna have it on the trail

874
00:37:56.090 --> 00:37:58.203
when there's many people out there,

875
00:37:59.412 --> 00:38:01.303
you got pass people and stuff like that

876
00:38:01.303 --> 00:38:03.210
on a little single track.

877
00:38:03.210 --> 00:38:05.590
So the music is really powerful,

878
00:38:05.590 --> 00:38:09.920
because if I'm feeling discomfort, it numbs that.

879
00:38:09.920 --> 00:38:12.243
So I don't take any painkillers, ever.

880
00:38:13.530 --> 00:38:18.140
I haven't taken any since I broke my neck back in 2004

881
00:38:21.510 --> 00:38:24.010
so having the music actually helps me

882
00:38:24.010 --> 00:38:26.030
to put my mind somewhere else.

883
00:38:26.030 --> 00:38:29.750
If I am feeling like just total fatigue or discomfort,

884
00:38:29.750 --> 00:38:31.770
just listen to music,

885
00:38:31.770 --> 00:38:34.180
it's really nice to have it on the nighttime.

886
00:38:34.180 --> 00:38:37.280
What is the nutrition strategy?

887
00:38:37.280 --> 00:38:39.000
How are you keeping calories down

888
00:38:39.000 --> 00:38:40.920
after all that time out there?

889
00:38:40.920 --> 00:38:42.243
What's the feeling like?

890
00:38:42.243 --> 00:38:44.610
Well you just have to look at it

891
00:38:44.610 --> 00:38:47.760
as this is all you can eat buffet and be so thankful

892
00:38:47.760 --> 00:38:49.940
that you could eat all the food in the world.

893
00:38:49.940 --> 00:38:51.840
But it's really important to eat.

894
00:38:51.840 --> 00:38:55.840
Like probably 500 calories, 400, 500 calories an hour.

895
00:38:55.840 --> 00:38:56.680
That's a lot.

896
00:38:56.680 --> 00:38:58.100
It's a lot.
That's a lot to keep down.

897
00:38:58.100 --> 00:38:59.040
Yeah, it is.

898
00:38:59.040 --> 00:39:02.570
And that's actually where the plant-based foods

899
00:39:02.570 --> 00:39:03.623
really helps out,

900
00:39:04.540 --> 00:39:09.510
because I feel like I'm able to digest a lot of food,

901
00:39:09.510 --> 00:39:13.370
like a ton of food and drinks without having...

902
00:39:13.370 --> 00:39:18.230
Like, I very, very rarely have any sort of upset stomach

903
00:39:18.230 --> 00:39:20.570
in races, it's like extremely rare.

904
00:39:20.570 --> 00:39:24.400
So what's the combination of performance nutrition,

905
00:39:24.400 --> 00:39:28.700
like the high calorie drinks versus real food,

906
00:39:28.700 --> 00:39:30.220
and what does the real food look like?

907
00:39:30.220 --> 00:39:32.437
Right, so I would drink Tailwind,

908
00:39:32.437 --> 00:39:34.520
but I also had diversity.

909
00:39:34.520 --> 00:39:39.520
So I would have soda as well, I had like coconut water,

910
00:39:40.910 --> 00:39:42.639
which was really nice.

911
00:39:42.639 --> 00:39:45.310
And then I'd have like things from fruits,

912
00:39:45.310 --> 00:39:50.310
like watermelon, blueberries to squeezable fruits,

913
00:39:51.210 --> 00:39:56.210
to soups and like vegan mac and cheese, and have like...

914
00:39:57.680 --> 00:40:00.120
Judd was actually really good about making up food.

915
00:40:00.120 --> 00:40:02.200
That's another thing about your crew chief.

916
00:40:02.200 --> 00:40:04.840
Like with this ratio only allow one crew chief

917
00:40:04.840 --> 00:40:06.210
or one person in his crew.

918
00:40:06.210 --> 00:40:08.150
So it's really important to have someone

919
00:40:08.150 --> 00:40:10.450
who is able to make up food for you

920
00:40:10.450 --> 00:40:12.323
that knows a little bit about it.

921
00:40:13.220 --> 00:40:15.483
He's not vegan, but he's really good.

922
00:40:17.063 --> 00:40:19.390
Half the times, he makes me special things,

923
00:40:19.390 --> 00:40:21.010
brings it to the race (indistinct).

924
00:40:21.010 --> 00:40:23.230
Thank you so much.

925
00:40:23.230 --> 00:40:26.190
So if you're not getting 500 calories an hour on fruit,

926
00:40:26.190 --> 00:40:27.800
well, that's coming through Tailwind

927
00:40:27.800 --> 00:40:29.483
and through mac and cheese and stuff like that?

928
00:40:29.483 --> 00:40:32.850
Yeah and also like Abe's muffins from whole foods.

929
00:40:32.850 --> 00:40:34.430
I grab a bunch of those.

930
00:40:34.430 --> 00:40:36.550
Those are like actually believe it or not,

931
00:40:36.550 --> 00:40:39.420
it's like 400 calories or some 300 calories

932
00:40:39.420 --> 00:40:42.890
per little muffins and chips.

933
00:40:42.890 --> 00:40:45.900
So I'm eating guacamole and I'm eating a lot of things

934
00:40:45.900 --> 00:40:48.860
that aren't healthy to eat on a daily basis,

935
00:40:48.860 --> 00:40:53.750
just high calorie items, and not really looking too much

936
00:40:53.750 --> 00:40:56.790
at my protein content.

937
00:40:56.790 --> 00:41:00.440
It's more like wanna get complex carbohydrates

938
00:41:00.440 --> 00:41:02.280
rather than just simple sugars.

939
00:41:02.280 --> 00:41:05.220
Like I definitely am getting a ton of simple sugars,

940
00:41:05.220 --> 00:41:07.710
but I'm also getting complex carbohydrates too.

941
00:41:07.710 --> 00:41:11.440
So I mean everything like pastas, soups,

942
00:41:11.440 --> 00:41:12.860
things that are easy to digest.

943
00:41:12.860 --> 00:41:17.000
Like I'd have mashed potatoes

944
00:41:17.000 --> 00:41:20.229
and vegetable broth mixed together.

945
00:41:20.229 --> 00:41:22.790
And that was really easy to digest.

946
00:41:22.790 --> 00:41:24.440
And especially if it's hot out,

947
00:41:24.440 --> 00:41:27.830
then you wanna think about things that are easier to digest

948
00:41:27.830 --> 00:41:32.033
then at nighttime I could eat like the hardy items.

949
00:41:33.840 --> 00:41:35.200
No stomach upset?

950
00:41:35.200 --> 00:41:37.100
No stomach upset.
That's amazing.

951
00:41:38.729 --> 00:41:40.450
I think that it gets the best of them.

952
00:41:40.450 --> 00:41:41.740
Yeah, it's huge.

953
00:41:41.740 --> 00:41:46.740
The Ultra has 10 or 12 major components I can think of

954
00:41:49.110 --> 00:41:51.140
that are really important elements to race.

955
00:41:51.140 --> 00:41:53.700
Like pacing, pacing is huge,

956
00:41:53.700 --> 00:41:56.690
but nutrition is definitely one of the top ones.

957
00:41:56.690 --> 00:42:01.170
And like being able to just eat a bean burrito

958
00:42:01.170 --> 00:42:03.450
and then just take off,

959
00:42:03.450 --> 00:42:06.470
that's a huge advantage to be able to digest that

960
00:42:06.470 --> 00:42:08.040
while you're moving and running,

961
00:42:08.040 --> 00:42:09.490
it's a big advantage.

962
00:42:09.490 --> 00:42:10.760
But also Ultras are famous for like,

963
00:42:10.760 --> 00:42:15.380
"Oh, I'm just going to eat pizza, and all kinds of candy,

964
00:42:15.380 --> 00:42:16.810
and crap and stuff like that,

965
00:42:16.810 --> 00:42:18.900
because I'm burning all these calories out there

966
00:42:18.900 --> 00:42:21.310
and then people start barfing,

967
00:42:21.310 --> 00:42:24.016
and having the runs and all kinds of stuff happens.

968
00:42:24.016 --> 00:42:27.330
Yeah, I think that's where not having the dairy in there,

969
00:42:27.330 --> 00:42:28.633
really helps a lot too.

970
00:42:32.910 --> 00:42:34.770
I can name it like maybe three or four times

971
00:42:34.770 --> 00:42:35.820
in the last five years

972
00:42:35.820 --> 00:42:38.250
where I've actually gotten sick in Ultra, that's it.

973
00:42:38.250 --> 00:42:40.110
Like literally like three or four times.

974
00:42:40.110 --> 00:42:41.710
And usually I can explain what happened

975
00:42:41.710 --> 00:42:43.073
in each of those instances.

976
00:42:44.855 --> 00:42:48.493
I have to ask you, have you talked to Courtney about this?

977
00:42:48.493 --> 00:42:49.910
Does she has her...

978
00:42:49.910 --> 00:42:51.900
She served notorious for like her-

979
00:42:51.900 --> 00:42:53.170
Oh, the nachos and cheese?

980
00:42:53.170 --> 00:42:54.700
Like all the stuff that she eats.

981
00:42:54.700 --> 00:42:55.533
Right, right, right.

982
00:42:55.533 --> 00:42:58.780
And she occasionally has like a stomach issue

983
00:42:58.780 --> 00:43:00.294
in her races, right?
Yeah.

984
00:43:00.294 --> 00:43:01.900
And like there's Harvey, he's right over there.

985
00:43:01.900 --> 00:43:03.130
I love Courtney.

986
00:43:03.130 --> 00:43:04.450
I'm like very respectful about-

987
00:43:04.450 --> 00:43:07.310
Of course, I know, I'm not taking anybody's inventory.

988
00:43:07.310 --> 00:43:09.450
It's like she's in a ton of different planet.

989
00:43:09.450 --> 00:43:11.750
So she's like, knock yourself out, Courtney,

990
00:43:11.750 --> 00:43:12.700
whatever works for you.

991
00:43:12.700 --> 00:43:14.930
Exactly, so-
Only love.

992
00:43:14.930 --> 00:43:15.763
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

993
00:43:15.763 --> 00:43:20.580
So I usually am kind of careful about how I...

994
00:43:21.990 --> 00:43:23.780
If someone asks me then I'll share,

995
00:43:23.780 --> 00:43:25.430
but I don't usually like...

996
00:43:27.120 --> 00:43:29.122
Once in a while I do,

997
00:43:29.122 --> 00:43:31.010
but we do share ideas.

998
00:43:31.010 --> 00:43:33.610
A lot of times, like on The 24 Hour Team,

999
00:43:33.610 --> 00:43:35.213
we'll have something that works really well

1000
00:43:35.213 --> 00:43:37.430
and the other runners will pick up on it.

1001
00:43:37.430 --> 00:43:41.400
So I imagine I'd love to collaborate with her more

1002
00:43:41.400 --> 00:43:42.233
in the future,

1003
00:43:44.080 --> 00:43:47.665
but I haven't really said a whole lot to her about it.

1004
00:43:47.665 --> 00:43:49.300
Yeah, completely.

1005
00:43:49.300 --> 00:43:50.590
I wouldn't either.

1006
00:43:50.590 --> 00:43:53.820
She's got her nachos and cheese that she loves.

1007
00:43:53.820 --> 00:43:56.205
If that's working for her, awesome.

1008
00:43:56.205 --> 00:43:59.710
Maybe this is a good opportunity

1009
00:43:59.710 --> 00:44:02.666
to dive a little bit deeper into the whole vegan thing.

1010
00:44:02.666 --> 00:44:05.623
You went a hundred percent plant-based in 2016.

1011
00:44:07.000 --> 00:44:07.833
Yes.

1012
00:44:07.833 --> 00:44:10.380
So talk me through what that was about

1013
00:44:10.380 --> 00:44:11.710
and why you made that switch

1014
00:44:11.710 --> 00:44:13.180
and what you were doing beforehand.

1015
00:44:13.180 --> 00:44:15.410
Right, so going back in time,

1016
00:44:15.410 --> 00:44:19.600
I've actually been vegetarian since 25 years ago

1017
00:44:19.600 --> 00:44:22.900
when I first picked up Ultras at the same time.

1018
00:44:22.900 --> 00:44:27.410
And that was just a revolution my life.

1019
00:44:27.410 --> 00:44:31.193
Just because I felt a transition just overnight.

1020
00:44:33.280 --> 00:44:35.620
I went from being like Chunk in Goonies,

1021
00:44:35.620 --> 00:44:38.280
like the chubby kid growing up

1022
00:44:38.280 --> 00:44:43.280
and not being like one of the top runners at all.

1023
00:44:43.570 --> 00:44:47.220
In fact, I was usually one of the last runners

1024
00:44:47.220 --> 00:44:48.760
to finish races.

1025
00:44:48.760 --> 00:44:49.864
And so-

1026
00:44:49.864 --> 00:44:51.470
Yeah, I think it took you, like you said,

1027
00:44:51.470 --> 00:44:53.680
it took you years and years and years to break five hours

1028
00:44:53.680 --> 00:44:54.513
in the marathon.

1029
00:44:54.513 --> 00:44:55.460
Yeah, it took me five years

1030
00:44:55.460 --> 00:44:58.143
to actually break five hours in the marathon.

1031
00:44:58.143 --> 00:45:00.680
I did get started young, at 15,

1032
00:45:00.680 --> 00:45:04.570
but it's amazing for me mentally,

1033
00:45:04.570 --> 00:45:09.050
just to think about chiseling through the little wall,

1034
00:45:09.050 --> 00:45:10.440
like the count of Monte Cristo,

1035
00:45:10.440 --> 00:45:13.150
just all the little walls I had to chisel through

1036
00:45:13.150 --> 00:45:13.983
to get here.

1037
00:45:13.983 --> 00:45:16.717
I mean, you've been chiseling for a long time.

1038
00:45:16.717 --> 00:45:19.560
You are like the ultimate,

1039
00:45:19.560 --> 00:45:22.060
like Harvard Business School test case

1040
00:45:22.060 --> 00:45:27.060
in like persistence and consistency, 25 years of relentless.

1041
00:45:27.290 --> 00:45:29.500
You really haven't taken a break, right?

1042
00:45:29.500 --> 00:45:31.660
You've just been doing this full speed.

1043
00:45:31.660 --> 00:45:33.800
You have a full-time career and we'll talk about that.

1044
