So I think I really believe in sleep

1549
01:07:34.450 --> 01:07:36.540
and I think it's important.

1550
01:07:36.540 --> 01:07:40.040
If you look at the top marathoners in the world,

1551
01:07:40.040 --> 01:07:42.860
they actually are getting like nine or 10 hours of sleep

1552
01:07:42.860 --> 01:07:44.440
and a nap in a day.

1553
01:07:44.440 --> 01:07:45.610
I mean, if I wasn't working,

1554
01:07:45.610 --> 01:07:48.277
I would probably take a nap every day too.

1555
01:07:48.277 --> 01:07:50.100
I think it's a great thing.

1556
01:07:50.100 --> 01:07:55.100
But I think getting seven or more hours of sleep

1557
01:07:55.110 --> 01:07:56.060
is really important.

1558
01:07:56.060 --> 01:07:59.320
Like as important as whatever you're doing physically.

1559
01:07:59.320 --> 01:08:01.170
I think it's really important to you.

1560
01:08:02.610 --> 01:08:04.160
Government is what you teach, right?

1561
01:08:04.160 --> 01:08:06.743
Yeah, I teach government, AP government,

1562
01:08:08.010 --> 01:08:10.990
and that's why I was really excited to listen to Andrew Yang

1563
01:08:10.990 --> 01:08:12.760
and your podcast with him.

1564
01:08:12.760 --> 01:08:14.045
Yeah, what do you think?

1565
01:08:14.045 --> 01:08:15.010
(indistinct) Yang gang?

1566
01:08:15.010 --> 01:08:18.973
Well, I was very intrigued by him, I love his energy.

1567
01:08:18.973 --> 01:08:23.142
I think he brings a strong dynamic that we need.

1568
01:08:23.142 --> 01:08:24.370
I feel like we need that voice.

1569
01:08:24.370 --> 01:08:29.370
And I know I have students that are attracted to him as well

1570
01:08:30.470 --> 01:08:31.743
as like a candidate.

1571
01:08:32.960 --> 01:08:36.860
But yeah, I think we need a change,

1572
01:08:38.890 --> 01:08:40.780
we definitely need to change in America.

1573
01:08:40.780 --> 01:08:42.100
There's no doubt about it.

1574
01:08:42.100 --> 01:08:45.540
We need like some positive energy infused

1575
01:08:45.540 --> 01:08:46.790
and that's one of the things with running.

1576
01:08:46.790 --> 01:08:48.630
Like I really like the fact that running

1577
01:08:48.630 --> 01:08:50.440
brings us together.

1578
01:08:50.440 --> 01:08:51.510
I don't think about someone

1579
01:08:51.510 --> 01:08:53.210
who's being Republican or Democrat.

1580
01:08:53.210 --> 01:08:57.873
I have friends of both extremes and it's like running,

1581
01:08:57.873 --> 01:08:59.967
Sometimes we get on topics and we're like,

1582
01:08:59.967 --> 01:09:02.120
"Okay, let's switch a topic here."

1583
01:09:02.120 --> 01:09:07.120
But yeah, I like that running brings us together.

1584
01:09:07.440 --> 01:09:12.320
Yeah, it has an amazing power to do that.

1585
01:09:12.320 --> 01:09:16.710
To just unite the best of us and bring us together in a way

1586
01:09:16.710 --> 01:09:18.580
that few other things can.

1587
01:09:18.580 --> 01:09:22.040
And it's beautiful for that and for so many other things,

1588
01:09:22.040 --> 01:09:26.260
but one of the reasons I asked you about teaching government

1589
01:09:26.260 --> 01:09:28.440
is that you've done a couple of cool things

1590
01:09:28.440 --> 01:09:31.470
with your running that I think are really unique,

1591
01:09:31.470 --> 01:09:33.470
in and outside of the Ultra space,

1592
01:09:33.470 --> 01:09:36.610
like you went and ran Gandhi's Salt March in 2008,

1593
01:09:36.610 --> 01:09:39.860
and then you did the Selma to Montgomery route in 2009.

1594
01:09:39.860 --> 01:09:44.200
So it's this cool way of applying,

1595
01:09:44.200 --> 01:09:46.530
a really practical civics lesson

1596
01:09:46.530 --> 01:09:48.350
and melding it with your running

1597
01:09:48.350 --> 01:09:51.390
to have a boots on the ground experience

1598
01:09:51.390 --> 01:09:56.390
of what those historical occurrences must've felt like.

1599
01:09:57.250 --> 01:09:58.393
Absolutely, yeah.

1600
01:09:59.679 --> 01:10:02.340
I really believe in experiential learning.

1601
01:10:02.340 --> 01:10:06.570
I would love to take every one of my students to Selma,

1602
01:10:06.570 --> 01:10:10.109
to do the summit trail every year.

1603
01:10:10.109 --> 01:10:10.942
That would be incredible,

1604
01:10:10.942 --> 01:10:12.853
but we're limited by time and resources.

1605
01:10:14.341 --> 01:10:15.820
So having the opportunity to go down there

1606
01:10:15.820 --> 01:10:18.500
with a group of 10 friends...

1607
01:10:18.500 --> 01:10:19.980
The second time I went down there

1608
01:10:19.980 --> 01:10:23.730
and to bring that back and share that with students...

1609
01:10:23.730 --> 01:10:26.390
When I was down there the first time in 2000,

1610
01:10:26.390 --> 01:10:28.790
I think it was seven.

1611
01:10:28.790 --> 01:10:31.200
I got to actually meet with civil rights leaders

1612
01:10:32.380 --> 01:10:36.530
like Mother Cooper, Amelia Boynton Robinson,

1613
01:10:36.530 --> 01:10:41.100
these folks were reaching a hundred years of age.

1614
01:10:41.100 --> 01:10:44.700
And so to get a chance to actually speak with them

1615
01:10:44.700 --> 01:10:48.140
and interview them about their experiences,

1616
01:10:48.140 --> 01:10:52.610
Harry Belafonte, to chat with these people

1617
01:10:54.273 --> 01:10:57.160
and really get pieces of the story that you don't read about

1618
01:10:57.160 --> 01:10:59.570
in the textbook, that was powerful.

1619
01:10:59.570 --> 01:11:02.570
To bring that back and share that with my students

1620
01:11:02.570 --> 01:11:07.570
and say, "Look, this is what I heard from Mother Cooper

1621
01:11:07.887 --> 01:11:09.737
"and her experience with the sheriff,

1622
01:11:10.607 --> 01:11:14.537
"what was like when they were trying to overcome, you know,

1623
01:11:14.537 --> 01:11:15.800
"the literacy tests."

1624
01:11:15.800 --> 01:11:19.050
That just makes it breathe life

1625
01:11:19.050 --> 01:11:20.940
into what you're trying to teach about

1626
01:11:20.940 --> 01:11:23.980
when you talk about the 1965 voting rights act

1627
01:11:23.980 --> 01:11:25.156
and I love that.

1628
01:11:25.156 --> 01:11:28.103
I would love to do that for every unit I have.

1629
01:11:29.630 --> 01:11:30.990
And then the Gandhi experience,

1630
01:11:30.990 --> 01:11:32.180
that was a whole nother story,

1631
01:11:32.180 --> 01:11:34.700
that when we could probably spend a few hours on,

1632
01:11:34.700 --> 01:11:39.700
but Gandhi, is an individual that I deeply admire.

1633
01:11:42.220 --> 01:11:45.360
So many facets of what he was able to achieve

1634
01:11:45.360 --> 01:11:48.080
based on the resources he had.

1635
01:11:48.080 --> 01:11:52.570
And following the 240-mile journey,

1636
01:11:52.570 --> 01:11:55.650
I start off my cousin and his girlfriend.

1637
01:11:55.650 --> 01:11:58.140
They broke up on the second day

1638
01:11:58.140 --> 01:12:01.920
and they ended up going cruise the head.

1639
01:12:01.920 --> 01:12:03.250
Eventually they ended up getting married,

1640
01:12:03.250 --> 01:12:04.960
so it all worked out.

1641
01:12:04.960 --> 01:12:07.010
But sign up by myself,

1642
01:12:07.010 --> 01:12:10.100
out there in India from day two, just carrying...

1643
01:12:10.100 --> 01:12:13.036
I thought, well, if Gandhi could survive with 10 items,

1644
01:12:13.036 --> 01:12:14.470
I should be able to like survive a month

1645
01:12:14.470 --> 01:12:16.420
with 10 items as well.

1646
01:12:16.420 --> 01:12:19.300
So I'm just running through the countryside

1647
01:12:19.300 --> 01:12:23.960
in these super intensely urban, very polluted cities,

1648
01:12:23.960 --> 01:12:28.380
Unfortunately the air pollution, there was really strong.

1649
01:12:28.380 --> 01:12:30.160
It's a lovely, lovely country.

1650
01:12:30.160 --> 01:12:33.090
And the people I met were so generous.

1651
01:12:33.090 --> 01:12:35.423
I met farmers that didn't have anything,

1652
01:12:36.730 --> 01:12:39.180
but they would want to offer something to me,

1653
01:12:39.180 --> 01:12:41.490
offer food or whatever they could give me.

1654
01:12:41.490 --> 01:12:42.883
And they were so generous.

1655
01:12:44.140 --> 01:12:47.580
The generosity I experienced amongst the people in India.

1656
01:12:47.580 --> 01:12:49.810
And then it kind of took off while I was there too.

1657
01:12:49.810 --> 01:12:51.290
Like they were writing about

1658
01:12:51.290 --> 01:12:53.490
in the "Times of India" every day,

1659
01:12:53.490 --> 01:12:56.070
and so every other truck driver

1660
01:12:56.070 --> 01:12:58.680
would be honking at me and waving.

1661
01:12:58.680 --> 01:13:03.680
And it was tough, because it was so hot,

1662
01:13:04.300 --> 01:13:05.780
it was like a hundred degrees,

1663
01:13:05.780 --> 01:13:07.657
but the humidity was really hot

1664
01:13:07.657 --> 01:13:11.060
and I wasn't as strong as I am now.

1665
01:13:11.060 --> 01:13:16.060
And so I even had blood in my urine on the third day,

1666
01:13:16.330 --> 01:13:19.850
which I've never had ever before or since,

1667
01:13:19.850 --> 01:13:23.330
it was just from overheating myself

1668
01:13:23.330 --> 01:13:25.223
and pushing myself too hard.

1669
01:13:27.110 --> 01:13:28.210
I destroyed my feet.

1670
01:13:28.210 --> 01:13:30.470
I had like a thousand blisters.

1671
01:13:30.470 --> 01:13:32.640
I had to get these bamboo sticks.

1672
01:13:32.640 --> 01:13:35.050
One day I found a big chunk of ice on the ground.

1673
01:13:35.050 --> 01:13:37.495
I was so thankful for that chunk of ice.

1674
01:13:37.495 --> 01:13:38.328
I was like, "It's so hot,

1675
01:13:38.328 --> 01:13:40.640
"oh, so you're gift from the heavens."

1676
01:13:40.640 --> 01:13:43.660
I've never been so excited about chunk of ice.

1677
01:13:43.660 --> 01:13:44.860
10 items?

1678
01:13:44.860 --> 01:13:45.995
Yeah.

1679
01:13:45.995 --> 01:13:46.830
You only brought 10 items with you?

1680
01:13:46.830 --> 01:13:49.430
So what were the 10 items and where you just camping?

1681
01:13:49.430 --> 01:13:50.330
Did you have a backpack

1682
01:13:50.330 --> 01:13:51.970
or was this like a self-supported thing?

1683
01:13:51.970 --> 01:13:54.030
Yeah, it was a self-supported,

1684
01:13:54.030 --> 01:13:55.968
I didn't really have a plan.

1685
01:13:55.968 --> 01:13:56.947
I was kind of just winging it,

1686
01:13:56.947 --> 01:13:59.510
and so I start off at the ganesh room

1687
01:13:59.510 --> 01:14:03.500
and they basically escorted me out city

1688
01:14:03.500 --> 01:14:05.620
with all these scooters

1689
01:14:05.620 --> 01:14:08.270
and they had the media there and everything.

1690
01:14:08.270 --> 01:14:12.050
And basically the first night or first couple of nights,

1691
01:14:12.050 --> 01:14:13.950
they organized for place for me to sleep.

1692
01:14:13.950 --> 01:14:16.770
But the first night I slept in the middle of this village,

1693
01:14:16.770 --> 01:14:21.770
just out on a bed, outside the small, small home.

1694
01:14:21.870 --> 01:14:24.150
And it was kinda funny 'cause I look up

1695
01:14:24.150 --> 01:14:26.120
and there was like 20 villagers circled around me

1696
01:14:26.120 --> 01:14:27.778
just looking down at me and I'm like,

1697
01:14:27.778 --> 01:14:30.440
"All right, can I sleep a little bit?."

1698
01:14:30.440 --> 01:14:33.340
But then I've met other people like this gentleman, Patel.

1699
01:14:36.690 --> 01:14:38.243
It was like the sixth day he invited me

1700
01:14:38.243 --> 01:14:40.750
to stay with his family at his house.

1701
01:14:40.750 --> 01:14:44.710
I just met him out on like this lonely road.

1702
01:14:44.710 --> 01:14:46.750
And he still keeps in touch with me today.

1703
01:14:46.750 --> 01:14:48.647
So like maybe once a month, he'll call me and say,

1704
01:14:48.647 --> 01:14:50.277
"How is your family?

1705
01:14:50.277 --> 01:14:52.483
"How is everything in Cincinnati?

1706
01:14:53.341 --> 01:14:54.291
"It's pretty neat."

1707
01:14:55.190 --> 01:15:00.190
But at the end we were met by a principal of a school

1708
01:15:01.140 --> 01:15:03.330
for deaf and blind children.

1709
01:15:03.330 --> 01:15:05.250
And they escorted me like the last mile.

1710
01:15:05.250 --> 01:15:09.620
So we shared that mile together, and that was super amazing.

1711
01:15:09.620 --> 01:15:12.740
Like I could never put a value on that,

1712
01:15:12.740 --> 01:15:15.690
sharing that experience with those kids was incredible.

1713
01:15:15.690 --> 01:15:20.430
And now when you think about Gandhi his civil disobedience

1714
01:15:20.430 --> 01:15:23.040
and what that Salt March must've been like,

1715
01:15:23.040 --> 01:15:28.000
you have a whole rich history of experience to draw upon

1716
01:15:28.000 --> 01:15:30.490
that makes that come to life in a way

1717
01:15:30.490 --> 01:15:32.760
that it can't in a textbook.

1718
01:15:32.760 --> 01:15:34.920
Yeah, absolutely.

1719
01:15:34.920 --> 01:15:39.800
I met a guy who shared stories about his father.

1720
01:15:39.800 --> 01:15:42.140
He brought me a sheet of paperwork.

1721
01:15:42.140 --> 01:15:44.560
Gandhi had written his father a note on it

1722
01:15:44.560 --> 01:15:49.560
and it was as much a connection with Gandhi's story

1723
01:15:52.680 --> 01:15:54.747
as it was the local people,

1724
01:15:54.747 --> 01:15:56.200
because the people were so...

1725
01:15:56.200 --> 01:15:59.090
Even though they didn't have a lot of material wealth,

1726
01:15:59.090 --> 01:16:03.420
they were extremely generous with their time

1727
01:16:03.420 --> 01:16:04.970
and whatever they had,

1728
01:16:04.970 --> 01:16:06.950
they would be willing to give it to you.

1729
01:16:06.950 --> 01:16:07.990
So a lot of times,

1730
01:16:07.990 --> 01:16:12.283
I'll stop in these tiny little like stores,

1731
01:16:13.140 --> 01:16:16.020
like a little shack that's selling goods

1732
01:16:16.020 --> 01:16:17.617
and they wouldn't let me pay and say,

1733
01:16:17.617 --> 01:16:19.317
"No, you're going to have to take that.

1734
01:16:19.317 --> 01:16:20.938
"You can't pay."

1735
01:16:20.938 --> 01:16:23.051
I was like, "Thank you."

1736
01:16:23.051 --> 01:16:24.000
Right, and they have nothing.

1737
01:16:24.000 --> 01:16:24.863
Yeah, nothing.

1738
01:16:25.738 --> 01:16:27.523
Wow.

1739
01:16:30.170 --> 01:16:33.953
You seem like a generally happy,

1740
01:16:35.730 --> 01:16:37.150
very positive guy.

1741
01:16:37.150 --> 01:16:39.653
Like you have this sunny disposition.

1742
01:16:40.880 --> 01:16:42.730
I'm looking at where's the dark side.

1743
01:16:43.624 --> 01:16:45.383
Is there a dark side?

1744
01:16:47.460 --> 01:16:48.293
When I look at you,

1745
01:16:48.293 --> 01:16:49.900
I see somebody who...

1746
01:16:49.900 --> 01:16:52.860
That disposition is really a big part

1747
01:16:52.860 --> 01:16:57.060
of how you're able to weather such mental toughness

1748
01:16:57.060 --> 01:16:59.810
when the chips are down in these races.

1749
01:16:59.810 --> 01:17:01.300
I see the same thing in Courtney.

1750
01:17:01.300 --> 01:17:05.890
She's like the world's a good place

1751
01:17:05.890 --> 01:17:09.410
and it's gonna be fine and let's have a good time.

1752
01:17:09.410 --> 01:17:12.483
And that's an under appreciated superpower.

1753
01:17:14.231 --> 01:17:15.131
It's how I feel.

1754
01:17:20.170 --> 01:17:23.740
I do have my moments,

1755
01:17:23.740 --> 01:17:26.290
but I try and get myself through those moments

1756
01:17:26.290 --> 01:17:30.283
by focusing on good things, like the good things.

1757
01:17:31.440 --> 01:17:33.540
There's a lot of rough things out there

1758
01:17:33.540 --> 01:17:36.700
that we can all get ourselves caught up in.

1759
01:17:36.700 --> 01:17:40.260
But focusing on the people in our circle,

1760
01:17:40.260 --> 01:17:45.260
focusing on the experiences that we can have in life,

1761
01:17:50.708 --> 01:17:53.120
it can be a superpower, I guess.

1762
01:17:53.120 --> 01:17:54.803
What kind of kid were you?

1763
01:17:56.000 --> 01:17:58.654
I was a challenging kid, I was a big challenging kid.

1764
01:17:58.654 --> 01:18:03.654
I have to go back to like my parents getting divorced

1765
01:18:05.250 --> 01:18:06.450
when I was like five.

1766
01:18:06.450 --> 01:18:10.440
So I grew up in this remote area of Pennsylvania

1767
01:18:10.440 --> 01:18:11.890
when I was really young,

1768
01:18:11.890 --> 01:18:14.420
a place called Chalkhill, Pennsylvania.

1769
01:18:14.420 --> 01:18:18.720
And then I moved to a suburb of Cleveland with my mother.

1770
01:18:18.720 --> 01:18:23.597
So had a challenge for me, well,

1771
01:18:23.597 --> 01:18:26.760
When I was going to school, I really struggled.

1772
01:18:26.760 --> 01:18:31.230
I was a D student, F student, all the way up

1773
01:18:31.230 --> 01:18:33.573
until like 10th grade.

1774
01:18:33.573 --> 01:18:35.230
I was really a struggling student.

1775
01:18:35.230 --> 01:18:40.230
I was literally placed in learning disability classes

1776
01:18:40.500 --> 01:18:42.223
because I was a problem-

1777
01:18:43.334 --> 01:18:45.590
'Cause you didn't care or you couldn't pay attention?

1778
01:18:45.590 --> 01:18:46.920
It was a variety of things.

1779
01:18:46.920 --> 01:18:49.163
I was a challenging with my behavior.

1780
01:18:50.365 --> 01:18:53.503
I'd do my own thing. (laughs)

1781
01:18:55.070 --> 01:18:58.750
Not like a bad kid, like I'm listening to anybody?

1782
01:18:58.750 --> 01:19:00.240
Yeah, I wasn't causing fires,

1783
01:19:00.240 --> 01:19:04.060
but yeah, I wouldn't listen.

1784
01:19:04.060 --> 01:19:07.860
I'd be often like challenging.

1785
01:19:07.860 --> 01:19:09.340
I just would be really challenging.

1786
01:19:09.340 --> 01:19:11.940
Is part of that your coping mechanism

1787
01:19:11.940 --> 01:19:15.925
for the trauma of the divorce, when you look back on that?

1788
01:19:15.925 --> 01:19:16.920
Probably some of it was because of that, a little bit.

1789
01:19:16.920 --> 01:19:18.870
And then also I think like learning styles,

1790
01:19:18.870 --> 01:19:21.140
like my learning style,

1791
01:19:21.140 --> 01:19:24.520
I got behind my first couple years of elementary school,

1792
01:19:24.520 --> 01:19:27.370
like saw I wasn't a good reader, I struggled.

1793
01:19:27.370 --> 01:19:30.810
I think that some of my teachers in the earlier grades

1794
01:19:30.810 --> 01:19:33.740
they'd won, like kind of pushed me off in the corner.

1795
01:19:33.740 --> 01:19:35.930
'Cause I was just, what do you do with this kid?

1796
01:19:35.930 --> 01:19:38.370
I've got a ton of stuff going on.

1797
01:19:38.370 --> 01:19:42.206
It's challenging to work with this student, you know?

1798
01:19:42.206 --> 01:19:43.563
And then you start to believe

1799
01:19:43.563 --> 01:19:45.760
that you're the kid who can't learn or.

1800
01:19:45.760 --> 01:19:49.410
Yeah, yeah and then I just had this mindset

1801
01:19:49.410 --> 01:19:51.023
that I just couldn't learn.

1802
01:19:51.956 --> 01:19:52.789
It was really true.

1803
01:19:52.789 --> 01:19:54.810
I had some amazing teachers along the road.

1804
01:19:54.810 --> 01:19:58.680
I had this Mrs. Ganesh,

1805
01:19:58.680 --> 01:20:01.700
with my elementary school teacher in special ed,

1806
01:20:01.700 --> 01:20:05.620
I really struggled with reading comprehension

1807
01:20:05.620 --> 01:20:08.000
and my self-efficacy.

1808
01:20:08.000 --> 01:20:11.100
So I really didn't think I could do it.

1809
01:20:11.100 --> 01:20:14.150
And I had a big, giant change

1810
01:20:14.150 --> 01:20:18.863
was when I went and did the marathon when I was 15.

1811
01:20:20.270 --> 01:20:21.493
What prompted that.

1812
01:20:23.047 --> 01:20:27.230
I've always been sort of a curious about challenges.

1813
01:20:27.230 --> 01:20:29.120
Like even when I was a chubby kid,

1814
01:20:29.120 --> 01:20:30.700
I was always kind of curious about...

1815
01:20:30.700 --> 01:20:33.710
I'd always wanna go climb up the hill.

1816
01:20:33.710 --> 01:20:36.690
That it doesn't look like it's very climbable

1817
01:20:36.690 --> 01:20:38.770
or do things like that.

1818
01:20:38.770 --> 01:20:41.513
And the marathon just intrigued me.

1819
01:20:43.260 --> 01:20:45.980
It cut into my morning cartoons when I was a kid,

1820
01:20:45.980 --> 01:20:47.420
like, I'd be watching on Saturday,

1821
01:20:47.420 --> 01:20:50.010
I'd be like, "What the heck is this?"

1822
01:20:50.010 --> 01:20:51.470
But I got drawn in,

1823
01:20:51.470 --> 01:20:54.380
I was watching it when I was probably like 11, 12,

1824
01:20:54.380 --> 01:20:56.090
somewhere around there, 13.

1825
01:20:56.090 --> 01:20:58.210
So I knew about this Cleveland marathon

1826
01:20:58.210 --> 01:21:01.640
and for whatever reason, I was like, I wanna do that,

1827
01:21:01.640 --> 01:21:04.430
I told my mom when I was like eight days,

1828
01:21:04.430 --> 01:21:07.057
nine days before the race, I wanna do that marathon.

1829
01:21:07.057 --> 01:21:10.980
And she was like, my mom always say okay to everything.

1830
01:21:10.980 --> 01:21:14.320
So she said, okay, we'll sign you up.

1831
01:21:14.320 --> 01:21:16.380
So I went down there and got signed up for the marathon.

1832
01:21:16.380 --> 01:21:19.173
The furthest I ran at that time was like eight miles.

1833
01:21:20.617 --> 01:21:22.166
And this is like, what?

1834
01:21:22.166 --> 01:21:23.380
Like you won?

1835
01:21:23.380 --> 01:21:26.030
Like 91, yeah, exactly, when I was 15.

1836
01:21:26.030 --> 01:21:28.670
So I was a freshman in high school.

1837
01:21:28.670 --> 01:21:30.650
My coach at the time said,

1838
01:21:30.650 --> 01:21:33.000
I ran one marathon and it was miserable.

1839
01:21:33.000 --> 01:21:35.331
There's no way you wanna do that.

1840
01:21:35.331 --> 01:21:38.050
(laughing) Thanks for the pep talk.

1841
01:21:38.050 --> 01:21:40.400
I know, and everyone else on the team is like, "Harvey,"

1842
01:21:40.400 --> 01:21:41.640
'cause I'd usually finish towards the back of the group.

1843
01:21:41.640 --> 01:21:43.997
They're like, "Harvey, there is no way

1844
01:21:43.997 --> 01:21:46.573
"you're gonna be able to finish that marathon."

1845
01:21:46.573 --> 01:21:48.740
And it's unusual then for somebody that young

1846
01:21:48.740 --> 01:21:50.270
to do something like that.
Back then.

1847
01:21:50.270 --> 01:21:52.240
The idea was you could harm yourself

1848
01:21:52.240 --> 01:21:55.090
or you get permanent damage.

1849
01:21:55.090 --> 01:21:57.250
So I end up going down

1850
01:21:57.250 --> 01:21:59.510
and I had to get down there by myself,

1851
01:21:59.510 --> 01:22:02.210
that digs mom was working at the hospital

1852
01:22:02.210 --> 01:22:04.960
and I ran the first ten miles.

1853
01:22:04.960 --> 01:22:08.270
Like I thought it was no problem at all.

1854
01:22:08.270 --> 01:22:09.880
I think I went real fast.

1855
01:22:09.880 --> 01:22:10.790
I got to mile 10,

1856
01:22:10.790 --> 01:22:13.477
I just about like, "Man, I'm a die,

1857
01:22:13.477 --> 01:22:15.510
"How in the world am I gonna even finish this thing?"

1858
01:22:15.510 --> 01:22:18.990
so I ran and walked the rest of the race.

1859
01:22:18.990 --> 01:22:21.140
And I met up with a couple of other runners

1860
01:22:22.320 --> 01:22:23.780
and that helped me out a lot.

1861
01:22:23.780 --> 01:22:26.920
And I ended up finishing that race.

1862
01:22:26.920 --> 01:22:29.360
I can still visualize the finish line.

1863
01:22:29.360 --> 01:22:31.500
And it just seemed like it was a country away.

1864
01:22:31.500 --> 01:22:33.200
Like I would never happen.

1865
01:22:33.200 --> 01:22:37.360
But finishing that race was monumental for me at the time,

1866
01:22:37.360 --> 01:22:41.440
because I had never experienced that kind of success.

1867
01:22:41.440 --> 01:22:44.530
So even though I finished it, like over five hours,

1868
01:22:44.530 --> 01:22:46.350
to me, just finishing the marathon

1869
01:22:46.350 --> 01:22:48.040
was a huge giant of success.

1870
01:22:48.040 --> 01:22:49.390
Especially when everyone was telling you

1871
01:22:49.390 --> 01:22:50.223
it was impossible.

1872
01:22:50.223 --> 01:22:51.780
Impossible, yeah.

1873
01:22:51.780 --> 01:22:55.450
So, that changed my mindset.

1874
01:22:55.450 --> 01:22:58.810
And then after that, I started applying myself in school.

1875
01:22:58.810 --> 01:23:02.660
And so, it would still take me probably two and a half,

1876
01:23:02.660 --> 01:23:04.550
three hours to do what someone would do

1877
01:23:04.550 --> 01:23:06.010
in like 30, 40 minutes,

1878
01:23:06.010 --> 01:23:07.430
like writing a paper,

1879
01:23:07.430 --> 01:23:10.040
but I put a lot energy and thought into it.

1880
01:23:10.040 --> 01:23:13.440
And then I end up really doing much better.

1881
01:23:13.440 --> 01:23:16.900
I end up like turning around from being a D student

1882
01:23:16.900 --> 01:23:18.670
that really didn't believe in themselves,

1883
01:23:18.670 --> 01:23:20.570
didn't wanna put themselves out there

1884
01:23:20.570 --> 01:23:23.360
for the risk of failure or whatever it might be,

1885
01:23:23.360 --> 01:23:25.867
or didn't think that I could do it to being like,

1886
01:23:25.867 --> 01:23:28.570
"I'm just gonna apply myself until I do do it."

1887
01:23:28.570 --> 01:23:32.210
So it was a monumental change for me.

1888
01:23:32.210 --> 01:23:33.830
That's really powerful to hear,

1889
01:23:33.830 --> 01:23:37.500
the idea of going out and doing something hard

1890
01:23:37.500 --> 01:23:40.160
and then learning as a young person

1891
01:23:40.160 --> 01:23:41.690
that you can apply that well,

1892
01:23:41.690 --> 01:23:45.240
or that determination to these other areas in your life.

1893
01:23:45.240 --> 01:23:48.700
And most people who get relegated to the side

1894
01:23:48.700 --> 01:23:49.960
in the educational system,

1895
01:23:49.960 --> 01:23:51.400
I don't have to tell you as a teacher,

1896
01:23:51.400 --> 01:23:52.970
like that's all she wrote.

1897
01:23:52.970 --> 01:23:55.140
It becomes very difficult for that person

1898
01:23:55.140 --> 01:23:57.120
to inch their way back in

1899
01:23:57.120 --> 01:24:00.230
and find a path to self-efficacy

1900
01:24:00.230 --> 01:24:04.300
short of finding something outside of the academic realm

1901
01:24:04.300 --> 01:24:06.000
that gives their life

1902
01:24:06.000 --> 01:24:08.563
sort of direction and purpose like you did.

1903
01:24:10.080 --> 01:24:13.570
Yeah, for our school at SCPA,

1904
01:24:13.570 --> 01:24:16.010
I think the arts do that for our kids.

1905
01:24:16.010 --> 01:24:18.600
Finding that vehicle through the arts

1906
01:24:18.600 --> 01:24:19.500
is super powerful,

1907
01:24:19.500 --> 01:24:23.410
but finding some vehicle to motivate kids

1908
01:24:23.410 --> 01:24:26.330
is revolutionary, is huge.

1909
01:24:26.330 --> 01:24:28.040
It's the whole game, man.

1910
01:24:28.040 --> 01:24:29.860
It's huge and it's hard.

1911
01:24:29.860 --> 01:24:33.030
I think that me having the background that I do,

1912
01:24:33.030 --> 01:24:35.010
where I really struggled through school,

1913
01:24:35.010 --> 01:24:37.550
And so like my sophomore junior year,

1914
01:24:37.550 --> 01:24:39.380
and then I really took off,

1915
01:24:39.380 --> 01:24:42.170
it does help me a bit with some of my kids

1916
01:24:42.170 --> 01:24:45.440
are like on that same edge where they're struggling

1917
01:24:45.440 --> 01:24:46.960
and I can kind of relate to them

1918
01:24:46.960 --> 01:24:51.960
and I can understand what it's like to not do well,

1919
01:24:52.610 --> 01:24:55.980
and to try to turn around.

1920
01:24:55.980 --> 01:24:57.080
Don't crack them though?

1921
01:24:57.080 --> 01:24:58.630
I don't always crack it.
It's tough.

1922
01:24:58.630 --> 01:25:02.560
It's very tough, but when you do with one or two,

1923
01:25:02.560 --> 01:25:05.805
or however that is, then it's like, wow,

1924
01:25:05.805 --> 01:25:07.055
that's like a wow moment.

1925
01:25:08.275 --> 01:25:10.530
Do you ever invite any of the students to run with you?

1926
01:25:10.530 --> 01:25:14.563
Oh yeah, we have a running club at school.

1927
01:25:15.690 --> 01:25:17.070
There's been different things-

1928
01:25:17.070 --> 01:25:18.480
It's an excuse for you to get a third run.

1929
01:25:18.480 --> 01:25:21.910
Yeah, it is, it is, it is.

1930
01:25:21.910 --> 01:25:26.063
But a number of students have really taken off with it.

1931
01:25:27.270 --> 01:25:28.490
One of my students, Logan,

1932
01:25:28.490 --> 01:25:30.747
he got into running clubs so much.

1933
01:25:30.747 --> 01:25:32.490
And then he ended up doing Ultras

1934
01:25:32.490 --> 01:25:34.570
and another one, Chris Meyer,

1935
01:25:34.570 --> 01:25:37.867
and is kind of neat to see them getting into it.

1936
01:25:37.867 --> 01:25:41.224
And actually Logan was at Badwater this year (indistinct)

1937
01:25:41.224 --> 01:25:42.270
one of our racers.

1938
01:25:42.270 --> 01:25:43.360
So that was really cool

1939
01:25:43.360 --> 01:25:45.550
to have happen.
That's really cool.

1940
01:25:45.550 --> 01:25:48.780
One time actually, one of my students,

1941
01:25:48.780 --> 01:25:53.023
'cause I was trying to really get his mind,

1942
01:25:54.070 --> 01:25:58.050
focus on his mind, but I actually raced him on his bus.

1943
01:25:58.050 --> 01:25:59.657
Like he took the bus home to his house,

1944
01:25:59.657 --> 01:26:01.830
and I raced, and I beat him.

1945
01:26:01.830 --> 01:26:03.757
I said, "I'll race you home.

1946
01:26:03.757 --> 01:26:06.610
"You take the bus, I'm a run, I'm a beat you.

1947
01:26:06.610 --> 01:26:08.230
He's like, "No way you gotta be..."

1948
01:26:08.230 --> 01:26:10.590
So I did it and that was fun.

1949
01:26:10.590 --> 01:26:11.870
So it was kind of like a breakthrough

1950
01:26:11.870 --> 01:26:14.283
'cause then we were able to build upon that.

1951
01:26:17.532 --> 01:26:22.180
But there's no cut science entirely with teaching.

1952
01:26:22.180 --> 01:26:26.943
You have to always be adapting and trying new things.

1953
01:26:28.287 --> 01:26:31.917
Well, that 14 to 18 kind of span of time,

1954
01:26:33.290 --> 01:26:36.260
which is where my parenting falls right now,

1955
01:26:36.260 --> 01:26:38.010
between two different kids

1956
01:26:38.010 --> 01:26:40.740
trying to figure out that way and is so tough.

1957
01:26:40.740 --> 01:26:43.550
And on some level it's like, it's not for me.

1958
01:26:43.550 --> 01:26:45.800
Like they don't wanna hear it from their parents.

1959
01:26:45.800 --> 01:26:49.320
It has to be through someone like you or somebody else,

1960
01:26:49.320 --> 01:26:51.380
that they can look to for inspiration.

1961
01:26:51.380 --> 01:26:54.070
But you know, try, I must. (laughs)

1962
01:26:54.070 --> 01:26:55.970
it's that age where it's like, they don't wanna hear it,

1963
01:26:55.970 --> 01:26:57.280
they don't wanna be around you.

1964
01:26:57.280 --> 01:26:58.600
it doesn't matter what you say,

1965
01:26:58.600 --> 01:26:59.660
it doesn't matter what you do,

1966
01:26:59.660 --> 01:27:00.980
you're just the furthest thing

1967
01:27:00.980 --> 01:27:03.730
from being influential on that level.

1968
01:27:03.730 --> 01:27:06.230
And it's a powerlessness that you feel as a parent

1969
01:27:08.433 --> 01:27:09.610
that makes it really hard.

1970
01:27:09.610 --> 01:27:12.910
It's just like, I can see here's where

1971
01:27:12.910 --> 01:27:14.360
I could wedge my way in

1972
01:27:14.360 --> 01:27:17.680
and maybe nudge this young person in this direction,

1973
01:27:17.680 --> 01:27:19.027
but they're not having it.

1974
01:27:19.027 --> 01:27:20.790
And they gotta find it for themselves

1975
01:27:20.790 --> 01:27:22.560
or they gotta hear it from somebody else.

1976
01:27:22.560 --> 01:27:25.797
Yeah, my son is 25 now.

1977
01:27:25.797 --> 01:27:28.190
And I went through that when he was a teenager.

1978
01:27:28.190 --> 01:27:30.960
It's like I tried every angle

1979
01:27:30.960 --> 01:27:35.090
and it's just like during those years we really struggled,

1980
01:27:35.090 --> 01:27:39.640
but I'm happy to say that now it's come full circle

1981
01:27:39.640 --> 01:27:44.640
is like really we have a much better communication now

1982
01:27:44.660 --> 01:27:47.820
than we did when he was like in those teenage years.

1983
01:27:47.820 --> 01:27:48.890
Yeah.
Yeah.

1984
01:27:48.890 --> 01:27:50.860
Well your relationship with your dad

1985
01:27:50.860 --> 01:27:52.840
is super interesting as well, right?

1986
01:27:52.840 --> 01:27:55.520
So the divorce happens.

1987
01:27:55.520 --> 01:27:58.530
You go to live with your mom and your-

1988
01:27:58.530 --> 01:28:00.220
I don't get the sense that you are a strange from your dad,

1989
01:28:00.220 --> 01:28:01.210
but you just didn't know him.

1990
01:28:01.210 --> 01:28:03.790
Like he wasn't in your daily experience, right?

1991
01:28:03.790 --> 01:28:07.510
Well, my dad was really good about communicating with me.

1992
01:28:07.510 --> 01:28:10.720
Like every Sunday we had a conversation

1993
01:28:10.720 --> 01:28:13.180
and it would be annoying to me at the time.

1994
01:28:13.180 --> 01:28:15.390
It'd feel like a lecture,

1995
01:28:15.390 --> 01:28:18.300
but it was also a way for us to stay connected

1996
01:28:18.300 --> 01:28:20.440
'cause he had moved to Minnesota,

1997
01:28:20.440 --> 01:28:22.352
fond the divorce.

1998
01:28:22.352 --> 01:28:24.670
And we also spent time in the summertime.

1999
01:28:24.670 --> 01:28:26.230
So we would typically...

2000
01:28:26.230 --> 01:28:28.140
He exposed me to actually it's a wilderness.

2001
01:28:28.140 --> 01:28:31.600
Like we would go when I was in seventh grade

2002
01:28:31.600 --> 01:28:34.990
and then also ninth going or maybe 10th grade,

2003
01:28:34.990 --> 01:28:37.930
we went to different trips out west

2004
01:28:37.930 --> 01:28:39.620
to the Wind River mountains.

2005
01:28:39.620 --> 01:28:42.460
So having that experience with my father,

2006
01:28:42.460 --> 01:28:46.150
even though it was limited on time, was super powerful

2007
01:28:46.150 --> 01:28:48.824
as well as valuable to me.

2008
01:28:48.824 --> 01:28:51.350
like I told you, I had the breakthrough with the marathon,

2009
01:28:51.350 --> 01:28:54.250
but there were other things that...

2010
01:28:54.250 --> 01:28:56.917
Having my parents in my camp were important.

2011
01:28:58.840 --> 01:28:59.673
A couple of years ago

2012
01:28:59.673 --> 01:29:02.500
you take a crack at the fastest known time

2013
01:29:02.500 --> 01:29:06.040
for the Appalachian trail and you use this also

2014
01:29:06.040 --> 01:29:10.280
as an experience to create a deeper connection with your dad

2015
01:29:10.280 --> 01:29:12.200
and you make this documentary out of the whole thing.

2016
01:29:12.200 --> 01:29:13.580
So talk a little bit about that,

2017
01:29:13.580 --> 01:29:16.550
'cause this is really super touching.

2018
01:29:16.550 --> 01:29:17.680
It's like a running thing,

2019
01:29:17.680 --> 01:29:18.670
but it's kind of not.

2020
01:29:18.670 --> 01:29:20.490
It's about something else altogether.

2021
01:29:20.490 --> 01:29:22.340
And I think it's really cool.

2022
01:29:22.340 --> 01:29:23.990
I haven't had a chance to watch the whole thing,

2023
01:29:23.990 --> 01:29:25.330
but I watched a couple,

2024
01:29:25.330 --> 01:29:27.360
I watched the trailer and then read up on it

2025
01:29:27.360 --> 01:29:28.240
and stuff like that.

2026
01:29:28.240 --> 01:29:30.490
So tell me more about this project.

2027
01:29:30.490 --> 01:29:31.951
Definitely.

2028
01:29:31.951 --> 01:29:33.760
So I came up with the idea

2029
01:29:33.760 --> 01:29:37.090
of going for the fastest known time on the 80.

2030
01:29:37.090 --> 01:29:42.090
And we set out for it in 2018 and I was looking at...

2031
01:29:42.510 --> 01:29:45.350
One of the biggest challenges is the logistics,

2032
01:29:45.350 --> 01:29:46.770
like who is going to crew you?

2033
01:29:46.770 --> 01:29:48.990
I mean, if you're going for a supportive record,

2034
01:29:48.990 --> 01:29:49.823
it's like 40...

2035
01:29:49.823 --> 01:29:52.510
It was 45 days at that time.

2036
01:29:52.510 --> 01:29:55.358
And like having someone with you for 45 days,

2037
01:29:55.358 --> 01:29:58.800
that's a major contribution, is a major commitment.

2038
01:29:58.800 --> 01:30:01.480
So I had a lot of friends that could come out

2039
01:30:01.480 --> 01:30:03.430
for maybe two or three days.

2040
01:30:03.430 --> 01:30:08.430
My friend Alan Lopez could come out for three weeks

2041
01:30:08.800 --> 01:30:11.610
but I needed someone who could be there the whole time.

2042
01:30:11.610 --> 01:30:14.900
And I don't know if I want to do this to my dad.

2043
01:30:14.900 --> 01:30:18.250
He's 79, that's a lot of intensity

2044
01:30:18.250 --> 01:30:20.010
to be driving on these rural roads.

2045
01:30:20.010 --> 01:30:22.550
And there are no road signs.

2046
01:30:22.550 --> 01:30:25.220
And like you have to navigate and stay up.

2047
01:30:25.220 --> 01:30:26.877
Who's in more apparel?

2048
01:30:26.877 --> 01:30:28.623
You on the trail or your dad in the van?

2049
01:30:28.623 --> 01:30:31.050
Exactly, I was really concerned on some days,

2050
01:30:31.050 --> 01:30:36.050
but my dad, he is a strong man.

2051
01:30:36.520 --> 01:30:40.777
Honestly, I don't know anyone as tough as he is

2052
01:30:40.777 --> 01:30:43.450
and a sense of his mental strength

2053
01:30:43.450 --> 01:30:46.460
and ability to problem solve.

2054
01:30:46.460 --> 01:30:48.133
He's got engineering background.

2055
01:30:51.093 --> 01:30:52.900
He said, "yeah, I'd like to come on out."

2056
01:30:52.900 --> 01:30:53.880
And it'd be a blast.

2057
01:30:53.880 --> 01:30:57.250
And then it became not just going for the record,

2058
01:30:57.250 --> 01:31:00.410
but actually having this opportunity

2059
01:31:00.410 --> 01:31:05.145
to spend 50 days with my father is like priceless.

2060
01:31:05.145 --> 01:31:05.978
You know what I mean?

2061
01:31:05.978 --> 01:31:08.610
