was I got into the Barkleys, so I get some more punished.

2359
01:44:17.330 --> 01:44:19.800
But you're being shamed for even talking about it.

2360
01:44:19.800 --> 01:44:20.790
It's like fight club.

2361
01:44:20.790 --> 01:44:22.230
Are you not allowed to say anything?

2362
01:44:22.230 --> 01:44:24.570
No, you can say a little bit about certain things,

2363
01:44:24.570 --> 01:44:26.500
but you can't say the date.

2364
01:44:26.500 --> 01:44:29.680
But like he has had challenges with a lot of people

2365
01:44:29.680 --> 01:44:32.107
or people showing up that aren't supposed to be there

2366
01:44:32.107 --> 01:44:35.100
and it's a relatively small space,

2367
01:44:35.100 --> 01:44:37.120
like at the campground and things like that.

2368
01:44:37.120 --> 01:44:40.220
So they don't want people showing up

2369
01:44:40.220 --> 01:44:44.703
like those dates and then lesser like invited guests.

2370
01:44:46.250 --> 01:44:49.990
Well, good news because we're heading into winter.

2371
01:44:49.990 --> 01:44:51.340
So you're going to get a fair amount

2372
01:44:51.340 --> 01:44:53.691
of cold weather in Cincinnati.

2373
01:44:53.691 --> 01:44:56.992
So I can see you out there shirtless.

2374
01:44:56.992 --> 01:44:58.269
I don't know if I'm gonna be there everyday,

2375
01:44:58.269 --> 01:45:03.269
but I might try like probably on my run commutes to school

2376
01:45:03.340 --> 01:45:06.580
a couple of days a week where I'll probably just wear

2377
01:45:06.580 --> 01:45:07.790
a T-shirt and shorts,

2378
01:45:07.790 --> 01:45:11.420
even if it's like 20 degrees or 15 degrees,

2379
01:45:11.420 --> 01:45:12.763
but not every day, not every day.

2380
01:45:12.763 --> 01:45:15.930
It's like with Badwater I only train for heat

2381
01:45:15.930 --> 01:45:17.070
a couple of days a week.

2382
01:45:17.070 --> 01:45:18.630
Like it's better for your mind.

2383
01:45:18.630 --> 01:45:20.730
And we might as well spend at least a minute on that.

2384
01:45:20.730 --> 01:45:22.900
'Cause I think, I know what you did,

2385
01:45:22.900 --> 01:45:24.520
but that would blow people's minds

2386
01:45:24.520 --> 01:45:28.820
to know the kind of heat acclimation work that you put in.

2387
01:45:28.820 --> 01:45:33.820
Badwater is definitely my favorite Ultras in the world.

2388
01:45:33.990 --> 01:45:36.590
It's not that far from your home.

2389
01:45:36.590 --> 01:45:38.390
So I know you've been out there.

2390
01:45:38.390 --> 01:45:40.863
I crewed it one year, that was enough for me.

2391
01:45:42.380 --> 01:45:44.790
I love the environment out there.

2392
01:45:44.790 --> 01:45:46.320
It's beautiful.
It's incredible.

2393
01:45:46.320 --> 01:45:51.020
So, I mean, it's 120 degrees typically in the race,

2394
01:45:51.020 --> 01:45:56.000
it's 135 miles, you go over three mountains

2395
01:45:56.000 --> 01:45:57.440
you finish on Whitney.

2396
01:45:57.440 --> 01:46:01.060
And yeah, this year was magical,

2397
01:46:01.060 --> 01:46:03.840
because it was my 10th year running Badwater.

2398
01:46:03.840 --> 01:46:06.530
I had won the race back in 2014

2399
01:46:06.530 --> 01:46:10.480
on the alternate course that year due to the park closure.

2400
01:46:10.480 --> 01:46:12.068
There was all that shenanigans.

2401
01:46:12.068 --> 01:46:16.110
So it was a dream of mine.

2402
01:46:16.110 --> 01:46:18.130
I write out goals each year,

2403
01:46:18.130 --> 01:46:20.960
and that was one of my 12 goals for the year,

2404
01:46:20.960 --> 01:46:22.580
was to win Badwater.

2405
01:46:22.580 --> 01:46:25.337
And it was wild because I had written it down.

2406
01:46:25.337 --> 01:46:30.337
And I mean, it was a bit of a high stretching goal,

2407
01:46:30.370 --> 01:46:32.880
but to have it come to fruition was, again,

2408
01:46:32.880 --> 01:46:35.040
it was just incredible because I've been dreaming

2409
01:46:35.040 --> 01:46:37.817
about that again for years and working at it

2410
01:46:37.817 --> 01:46:40.570
and just trying to tweak and tweak.

2411
01:46:40.570 --> 01:46:45.570
The race was very windy this year.

2412
01:46:45.710 --> 01:46:50.710
And there was a runner from Ukraine who got out ahead.

2413
01:46:53.080 --> 01:46:56.967
He was a leading throughout most of the race

2414
01:46:56.967 --> 01:46:59.700
and he was continuing to build a lead.

2415
01:46:59.700 --> 01:47:01.870
And then going up the second mountain,

2416
01:47:01.870 --> 01:47:04.073
this was about mile 79 to 83,

2417
01:47:07.610 --> 01:47:10.110
I was really feeling beat up.

2418
01:47:10.110 --> 01:47:10.943
Like I was actually-

2419
01:47:10.943 --> 01:47:13.046
That's the one where it's the middle of the night.

2420
01:47:13.046 --> 01:47:16.071
Because of the race starting at 10 PM-

2421
01:47:16.071 --> 01:47:17.690
Have they changed the race starting time?

2422
01:47:17.690 --> 01:47:20.640
Yeah, like last six years.

2423
01:47:20.640 --> 01:47:23.300
So that that's actually around noon,

2424
01:47:24.290 --> 01:47:26.380
like about noon or one o'clock.

2425
01:47:26.380 --> 01:47:28.830
And I was feeling like a bit beat up.

2426
01:47:28.830 --> 01:47:31.390
I was trying to hang in there mentally, I was thinking,

2427
01:47:31.390 --> 01:47:33.840
'Cause I always think like,

2428
01:47:33.840 --> 01:47:36.420
you never know what's gonna happen to a person in front.

2429
01:47:36.420 --> 01:47:38.717
And I was in second place at the time and I'm just like,

2430
01:47:38.717 --> 01:47:40.720
"I just have to work through this."

2431
01:47:40.720 --> 01:47:43.200
So my team getting Judd and my other friends,

2432
01:47:43.200 --> 01:47:44.930
they were trying to keep me hydrated,

2433
01:47:44.930 --> 01:47:46.480
give me like enough liquids,

2434
01:47:46.480 --> 01:47:47.933
I wasn't doing the greatest,

2435
01:47:49.860 --> 01:47:53.943
somehow I got to that aid station at Darwin.

2436
01:47:55.090 --> 01:48:00.090
I had this fire that hit me.

2437
01:48:00.510 --> 01:48:05.510
And I just felt like a different person.

2438
01:48:06.190 --> 01:48:11.050
And I knew that at that aid station,

2439
01:48:11.050 --> 01:48:13.400
I got the clock time on the runner in front

2440
01:48:13.400 --> 01:48:15.410
and I knew that he was struggling as well,

2441
01:48:15.410 --> 01:48:18.100
because despite me slowing down,

2442
01:48:18.100 --> 01:48:20.687
he had put no timer on me and I'm like,

2443
01:48:20.687 --> 01:48:22.190
"Okay, I'm a go get you."

2444
01:48:22.190 --> 01:48:24.180
What was the gap at that point?

2445
01:48:24.180 --> 01:48:28.950
It was about 25 minutes or so like I'm bound,

2446
01:48:28.950 --> 01:48:32.310
like I'm gonna just give everything I have.

2447
01:48:32.310 --> 01:48:33.730
And then, so we started running,

2448
01:48:33.730 --> 01:48:36.210
we ran a few miles and Elvis' sun,

2449
01:48:36.210 --> 01:48:38.320
we had this really special experience

2450
01:48:38.320 --> 01:48:42.600
where the fighter jets literally just buzzed over top of us,

2451
01:48:42.600 --> 01:48:45.350
like 60 meters above our heads.

2452
01:48:45.350 --> 01:48:49.580
And that just lit me up even more.

2453
01:48:49.580 --> 01:48:51.500
And now I'm just like, all right.

2454
01:48:51.500 --> 01:48:55.250
And then maybe another half a mile from that,

2455
01:48:55.250 --> 01:48:58.100
literally I saw Igor Ukrainian runner

2456
01:48:58.100 --> 01:48:59.570
and he was in my sights.

2457
01:48:59.570 --> 01:49:03.980
And then I just was very consistent not to over overdo it,

2458
01:49:03.980 --> 01:49:07.440
but to continually like chip away.

2459
01:49:07.440 --> 01:49:10.800
And I think I caught up to him about mile 114.

2460
01:49:10.800 --> 01:49:12.380
We had a really good exchange.

2461
01:49:12.380 --> 01:49:16.293
Actually, it was very like a positive between the two of us.

2462
01:49:17.280 --> 01:49:19.450
It was actually one of the coolest events,

2463
01:49:19.450 --> 01:49:20.581
moments I've ever had at Ronnie

2464
01:49:20.581 --> 01:49:23.240
to have like a positive embrace

2465
01:49:23.240 --> 01:49:25.147
and where I was like, "Keep at it."

2466
01:49:25.147 --> 01:49:26.790
And he's like, "You go get it."

2467
01:49:26.790 --> 01:49:29.593
And that was something really special.

2468
01:49:30.860 --> 01:49:34.090
Wow, two wins, 10 Badwaters

2469
01:49:34.090 --> 01:49:36.050
plus the fastest known time

2470
01:49:36.050 --> 01:49:39.600
from Badwater to the summit, Mount Whitney Summit.

2471
01:49:39.600 --> 01:49:43.156
That was another wild one, so it was 2020-

2472
01:49:43.156 --> 01:49:45.790
First of all, Badwater for people that don't know,

2473
01:49:45.790 --> 01:49:48.900
135 miles across the desert,

2474
01:49:48.900 --> 01:49:52.020
most people are familiar with the crazy heat and they know,

2475
01:49:52.020 --> 01:49:53.860
some sense of what that's like,

2476
01:49:53.860 --> 01:49:57.690
but they don't really understand that there's like an insane

2477
01:49:57.690 --> 01:50:00.766
amount of elevation gain and the last...

2478
01:50:00.766 --> 01:50:02.420
When you get into the portals

2479
01:50:02.420 --> 01:50:05.780
and you go up to the last 8,000 feet of Mount Whitney,

2480
01:50:05.780 --> 01:50:07.710
it's like the last, what is it?

2481
01:50:07.710 --> 01:50:09.620
Half marathon is like straight up hill.

2482
01:50:09.620 --> 01:50:12.770
Yeah, the last half marathon is definitely a climb.

2483
01:50:12.770 --> 01:50:16.690
It's like another 5,000 feet up to the portal.

2484
01:50:16.690 --> 01:50:19.170
But then it's a whole other thing altogether

2485
01:50:19.170 --> 01:50:22.110
to continue past that point

2486
01:50:22.110 --> 01:50:23.570
and go all the way to the summit,

2487
01:50:23.570 --> 01:50:25.380
which is the highest point in north America.

2488
01:50:25.380 --> 01:50:27.720
So the original concept behind this race,

2489
01:50:27.720 --> 01:50:29.750
was you go from the lowest point in north America

2490
01:50:29.750 --> 01:50:32.520
to the highest point in the contiguous US. Yeah.

2491
01:50:32.520 --> 01:50:33.430
Yeah, absolutely.

2492
01:50:33.430 --> 01:50:36.880
So Al Arnold did this back in 1976

2493
01:50:36.880 --> 01:50:41.880
and set it up 146 is the original Badwater course,

2494
01:50:42.490 --> 01:50:45.760
but the park service as things are...

2495
01:50:45.760 --> 01:50:47.529
They really didn't like the idea

2496
01:50:47.529 --> 01:50:49.930
of having runners go up Whitney, which makes sense.

2497
01:50:49.930 --> 01:50:50.763
You've run 135 Miles,

2498
01:50:50.763 --> 01:50:51.950
and now you're gonna do a summit push

2499
01:50:51.950 --> 01:50:53.807
and it's treacherous up there.

2500
01:50:53.807 --> 01:50:54.640
It's treacherous.
And people would

2501
01:50:54.640 --> 01:50:55.540
die for sure.

2502
01:50:55.540 --> 01:50:58.810
In the early days we're talking about 20 people

2503
01:50:58.810 --> 01:50:59.900
in the race.

2504
01:50:59.900 --> 01:51:01.550
So it wasn't like the bigger number.

2505
01:51:01.550 --> 01:51:03.600
Now we have a hundred people.

2506
01:51:03.600 --> 01:51:05.880
So I think that, runners,

2507
01:51:05.880 --> 01:51:09.440
we don't oftentimes put safety nets in there

2508
01:51:09.440 --> 01:51:10.870
or like stoppage.

2509
01:51:10.870 --> 01:51:12.100
So if it was lightening,

2510
01:51:12.100 --> 01:51:13.457
I could see some people would be like,

2511
01:51:13.457 --> 01:51:15.257
"Oh hell, I'm still going up there."

2512
01:51:16.338 --> 01:51:17.610
I'm so invested at this point.

2513
01:51:17.610 --> 01:51:22.110
Right, so it is good that we have the 135

2514
01:51:22.110 --> 01:51:24.770
with the number of runners we have today,

2515
01:51:24.770 --> 01:51:27.480
but in 2020 we are having the pandemic

2516
01:51:27.480 --> 01:51:29.980
and the race was canceled.

2517
01:51:29.980 --> 01:51:32.190
And I had trained for the race

2518
01:51:32.190 --> 01:51:34.469
and I'd never done the 146

2519
01:51:34.469 --> 01:51:36.320
and I was always intrigued about it.

2520
01:51:36.320 --> 01:51:38.090
And Marshall Orrick had the record

2521
01:51:38.090 --> 01:51:39.930
going back 29 years earlier.

2522
01:51:39.930 --> 01:51:41.300
I have lot of respect for Marshall.

2523
01:51:41.300 --> 01:51:42.133
91?

2524
01:51:42.133 --> 01:51:43.720
Same year you did that first marathon?

2525
01:51:43.720 --> 01:51:44.770
Same year, yeah.

2526
01:51:44.770 --> 01:51:46.123
He's a phenomenal guy.

2527
01:51:47.428 --> 01:51:50.247
So I thought, "Okay, I'm just gonna organize my team.

2528
01:51:50.247 --> 01:51:54.400
"We'll go out there in August and we'll go for it."

2529
01:51:54.400 --> 01:51:56.670
We raised money for the city gospel mission,

2530
01:51:56.670 --> 01:51:59.870
which is like a homeless shelter in Cincinnati

2531
01:51:59.870 --> 01:52:01.950
that also has a running program

2532
01:52:01.950 --> 01:52:03.980
for individuals that are recovering.

2533
01:52:03.980 --> 01:52:04.813
Oh, that's cool.

2534
01:52:04.813 --> 01:52:05.840
It's so cool.

2535
01:52:05.840 --> 01:52:07.147
And at first I was thinking,

2536
01:52:07.147 --> 01:52:10.320
"Okay, we'll just go for like a thousand dollars or $2,000."

2537
01:52:10.320 --> 01:52:13.360
I wasn't like really hyping it up that much.

2538
01:52:13.360 --> 01:52:15.960
And by the time we got out there,

2539
01:52:15.960 --> 01:52:19.670
we had like $5,000 were donated.

2540
01:52:19.670 --> 01:52:22.660
And so that actually kind of fueled my fire,

2541
01:52:22.660 --> 01:52:26.570
like going into that event at mile 17

2542
01:52:26.570 --> 01:52:28.637
to hear feedback from the team that,

2543
01:52:28.637 --> 01:52:30.537
"Hey, we had already raised 5,000."

2544
01:52:30.537 --> 01:52:32.190
And it was going up.

2545
01:52:32.190 --> 01:52:33.843
And it meant a lot to me.

2546
01:52:38.010 --> 01:52:41.290
I try to keep really a good pace going through the race.

2547
01:52:41.290 --> 01:52:46.290
When I got into lone pine at mile 122,

2548
01:52:46.660 --> 01:52:49.700
I felt, there's no way I'm gonna get this record.

2549
01:52:49.700 --> 01:52:53.620
I was just depleted after going through the desert,

2550
01:52:53.620 --> 01:52:55.093
I was exhausted.

2551
01:52:56.341 --> 01:52:59.667
And I was like, "Okay, well, we're gonna like go up

2552
01:52:59.667 --> 01:53:04.497
"to the 135 and we'll see how it goes.

2553
01:53:04.497 --> 01:53:06.910
"Maybe I'll just stop at that point."

2554
01:53:06.910 --> 01:53:09.960
And so I was hiking up the final 13 miles.

2555
01:53:09.960 --> 01:53:12.070
It would have been to the 135

2556
01:53:13.372 --> 01:53:16.027
and I was talking to my fiance on the phone and I was like,

2557
01:53:16.027 --> 01:53:20.067
"Kelly, I feel like, really, I don't think this is possible.

2558
01:53:20.067 --> 01:53:22.827
"I really feel like there's like a 1% chance

2559
01:53:22.827 --> 01:53:23.660
"I could do this.

2560
01:53:23.660 --> 01:53:26.370
"There's no chance I can do this, I feel depleted."

2561
01:53:26.370 --> 01:53:28.043
That's an unusual statement coming from you.

2562
01:53:28.043 --> 01:53:29.030
It's unusual.

2563
01:53:29.030 --> 01:53:30.240
Like you're saying it out loud.

2564
01:53:30.240 --> 01:53:31.900
Yeah, I'm saying it out loud,

2565
01:53:31.900 --> 01:53:34.290
is like 1% chance I can do this.

2566
01:53:34.290 --> 01:53:37.250
I feel like totally depleted right now.

2567
01:53:37.250 --> 01:53:39.768
And as I was going up the mountain,

2568
01:53:39.768 --> 01:53:44.230
with about maybe five miles before the portal,

2569
01:53:44.230 --> 01:53:47.000
the temperatures were starting to drop a bit

2570
01:53:47.000 --> 01:53:50.910
and I think I was able to get more liquids into my body

2571
01:53:50.910 --> 01:53:52.497
and I was starting to feel,

2572
01:53:52.497 --> 01:53:55.400
"Hm, okay, maybe there's something here."

2573
01:53:55.400 --> 01:53:58.090
And so when I got to the last two miles up to the portal,

2574
01:53:58.090 --> 01:53:59.883
I said to one of my friends, I say,

2575
01:54:01.507 --> 01:54:05.597
"Max, you're gonna have to prepare things,

2576
01:54:05.597 --> 01:54:08.570
"cause I think I might go for it."

2577
01:54:08.570 --> 01:54:13.570
So actually I said that to Jeremy.

2578
01:54:14.410 --> 01:54:17.551
So when we got to the top,

2579
01:54:17.551 --> 01:54:21.030
I still wasn't feeling like thunder,

2580
01:54:21.030 --> 01:54:25.650
but I felt like maybe I'm a go for this.

2581
01:54:25.650 --> 01:54:28.160
I don't know if it's still, I'm not sure if it's possible.

2582
01:54:28.160 --> 01:54:30.240
At that point when I got to a portal,

2583
01:54:30.240 --> 01:54:32.650
what was really amazing was Marshall Orrick

2584
01:54:32.650 --> 01:54:35.350
who had the record from 29 years earlier,

2585
01:54:35.350 --> 01:54:37.247
called me on the phone and he's like,

2586
01:54:37.247 --> 01:54:39.597
"Harvey, you know what?

2587
01:54:39.597 --> 01:54:41.520
"You can do this."

2588
01:54:41.520 --> 01:54:42.930
And I was thinking, I can't,

2589
01:54:42.930 --> 01:54:45.420
because I'm an hour behind where I wanna be

2590
01:54:45.420 --> 01:54:46.740
in order to make this ascend.

2591
01:54:46.740 --> 01:54:49.000
And I know Marshall's an amazing climber,

2592
01:54:49.000 --> 01:54:51.496
he's climbed all the seven summits.

2593
01:54:51.496 --> 01:54:55.820
But he gave me like a little 45 seconds,

2594
01:54:55.820 --> 01:54:58.720
60 seconds pep talk, which is amazing.

2595
01:54:58.720 --> 01:55:00.870
Someone has had that record for 29 years

2596
01:55:00.870 --> 01:55:03.020
to be so gracious to do.

2597
01:55:03.020 --> 01:55:03.870
It's super cool.

2598
01:55:03.870 --> 01:55:05.409
So cool.

2599
01:55:05.409 --> 01:55:07.679
How did you even have service up there?

2600
01:55:07.679 --> 01:55:08.733
How could you even get a phone call?

2601
01:55:08.733 --> 01:55:10.300
We get service at the portal

2602
01:55:10.300 --> 01:55:12.960
I don't know, my team, they put that together.

2603
01:55:12.960 --> 01:55:15.620
Like they had gotten in touch with him

2604
01:55:15.620 --> 01:55:20.550
and then my friend Michael Jimenez, and Davie McCoy

2605
01:55:20.550 --> 01:55:24.840
and his friend, they accompanied me up to the summit

2606
01:55:24.840 --> 01:55:26.660
as like a crew.

2607
01:55:26.660 --> 01:55:28.550
And like once I got on the trail,

2608
01:55:28.550 --> 01:55:31.310
like I had my hiking poles,

2609
01:55:31.310 --> 01:55:32.830
Marshall, and I had talked extensively

2610
01:55:32.830 --> 01:55:33.990
about what was permitted,

2611
01:55:33.990 --> 01:55:37.170
what was not permitted time, to leave everything.

2612
01:55:37.170 --> 01:55:40.840
And I felt like a new energy.

2613
01:55:40.840 --> 01:55:41.890
Like, again, it was wild.

2614
01:55:41.890 --> 01:55:44.780
Like I've felt different than I felt down below,

2615
01:55:44.780 --> 01:55:46.123
like I felt like, okay,

2616
01:55:47.034 --> 01:55:48.610
I felt I'm just gonna go with everything I got,

2617
01:55:48.610 --> 01:55:51.303
I don't know if we have a shot, but I was moving.

2618
01:55:51.303 --> 01:55:52.440
I was moving really fast.

2619
01:55:52.440 --> 01:55:54.890
And Michael said, "Wow, it was hard to keep up with you."

2620
01:55:54.890 --> 01:55:59.170
And so once I got to the switch,

2621
01:55:59.170 --> 01:56:00.720
once you get at the top of the switchbacks,

2622
01:56:00.720 --> 01:56:02.110
you'd come up over the ridge line

2623
01:56:02.110 --> 01:56:06.220
and there's like a 1.8 miles left or something like that.

2624
01:56:06.220 --> 01:56:08.257
Now I thought, "Okay, it's in our site,

2625
01:56:08.257 --> 01:56:11.580
"as long as I don't make any catastrophic mistakes."

2626
01:56:11.580 --> 01:56:15.163
And then to get there at like 3:30 in the morning,

2627
01:56:16.330 --> 01:56:20.290
that was like just incredibly powerful,

2628
01:56:20.290 --> 01:56:21.820
because on the Appalachian trail,

2629
01:56:21.820 --> 01:56:23.040
we ended up coming up short.

2630
01:56:23.040 --> 01:56:25.070
We got like the eighth fastest time,

2631
01:56:25.070 --> 01:56:28.200
which was still amazing because we got that.

2632
01:56:28.200 --> 01:56:31.090
It had an amazing experience, I could never take back,

2633
01:56:31.090 --> 01:56:32.870
but again, to get up there,

2634
01:56:32.870 --> 01:56:37.870
to be a part of Marshall's record from 29 years ago

2635
01:56:39.400 --> 01:56:41.140
and then to involve my team

2636
01:56:41.140 --> 01:56:43.713
and then to do it for the city gospel mission,

2637
01:56:44.830 --> 01:56:45.663
that was-

2638
01:56:45.663 --> 01:56:46.496
Yeah, you got it.

2639
01:56:46.496 --> 01:56:48.900
You clipped it by like 20 minutes or something like that.

2640
01:56:48.900 --> 01:56:51.827
But here's the thing you get up to the summit, you did it,

2641
01:56:51.827 --> 01:56:53.490
you still gotta get down.

2642
01:56:53.490 --> 01:56:54.323
Yeah, right.

2643
01:56:54.323 --> 01:56:56.310
You can't just get in a van and drive off.

2644
01:56:56.310 --> 01:56:57.544
That's the challenge.

2645
01:56:57.544 --> 01:56:59.113
The summit is a huge mountain.

2646
01:56:59.113 --> 01:57:02.020
My mind was really thinking about that,

2647
01:57:02.020 --> 01:57:04.950
because the temperature, even in the summertime,

2648
01:57:04.950 --> 01:57:06.930
is really cold up there.

2649
01:57:06.930 --> 01:57:08.770
And especially after you're like...

2650
01:57:08.770 --> 01:57:12.030
If you can think about if you've ran a marathon before

2651
01:57:12.030 --> 01:57:14.680
you've done some event where at the end of that race,

2652
01:57:14.680 --> 01:57:17.361
you get really chilled fast, like after-

2653
01:57:17.361 --> 01:57:19.590
That's where they handout those like tinfoil (indistinct).

2654
01:57:19.590 --> 01:57:22.710
Exactly, so like 10 minutes later, you get super chill.

2655
01:57:22.710 --> 01:57:23.880
So I went into the shelter,

2656
01:57:23.880 --> 01:57:26.000
I think for like five minutes, literally.

2657
01:57:26.000 --> 01:57:28.800
And then I was just all about focus,

2658
01:57:28.800 --> 01:57:30.600
but it took me a long time to get down.

2659
01:57:30.600 --> 01:57:32.850
Like actually I wasn't trying to speed down.

2660
01:57:32.850 --> 01:57:36.333
I was just taking my time, but it was slow.

2661
01:57:37.380 --> 01:57:38.510
I was doing-

2662
01:57:38.510 --> 01:57:43.070
146 miles in the legs, I think it's okay.

2663
01:57:43.070 --> 01:57:44.850
It's kind of amazing you're even...

2664
01:57:44.850 --> 01:57:48.410
The quads could even handle going down a mountain.

2665
01:57:48.410 --> 01:57:51.310
But the food after you have experience like that,

2666
01:57:51.310 --> 01:57:53.460
is the best food of your life.

2667
01:57:53.460 --> 01:57:55.660
That's one of the things about these Ultras,

2668
01:57:56.497 --> 01:57:59.030
is having that period where you're challenged

2669
01:58:03.690 --> 01:58:05.010
or some might call it suffering.

2670
01:58:05.010 --> 01:58:06.070
I would never call it suffering.

2671
01:58:06.070 --> 01:58:08.280
I like to look at differently in that,

2672
01:58:08.280 --> 01:58:10.390
but you're like in fatigue.

2673
01:58:10.390 --> 01:58:13.140
And then once you have the opportunity to like, relax,

2674
01:58:13.140 --> 01:58:16.600
it's like the best relaxation ever, get into the hot tub

2675
01:58:16.600 --> 01:58:19.080
or down at the bottom of a Whitney

2676
01:58:19.080 --> 01:58:21.070
is a really nice cold river

2677
01:58:21.070 --> 01:58:22.500
like a cold creek.

2678
01:58:22.500 --> 01:58:24.503
And I like to just get in that creek,

2679
01:58:26.610 --> 01:58:29.250
it feels like something magical.

2680
01:58:29.250 --> 01:58:32.210
It's like rejuvenating.

2681
01:58:32.210 --> 01:58:35.340
You earned it, buddy. (chuckles)

2682
01:58:35.340 --> 01:58:38.220
People are gonna kill me, if I don't ask you,

2683
01:58:38.220 --> 01:58:39.570
what you eat on the daily?

2684
01:58:39.570 --> 01:58:42.543
Like what's a day in the life of food for you.

2685
01:58:44.160 --> 01:58:45.003
How does that work?

2686
01:58:46.620 --> 01:58:48.730
During the weekdays, I'm in Cincinnati.

2687
01:58:48.730 --> 01:58:49.740
And then on the weekends,

2688
01:58:49.740 --> 01:58:52.463
I spend them with Kelly in Circleville.

2689
01:58:52.463 --> 01:58:54.320
So Circleville is a country town.

2690
01:58:54.320 --> 01:58:57.970
So Kelly makes some amazing foods.

2691
01:58:57.970 --> 01:59:00.550
On the weekdays,

2692
01:59:00.550 --> 01:59:04.970
I eat a lot of stuff that I just throw together.

2693
01:59:04.970 --> 01:59:09.230
Like breakfast might be like power owes and almond milk

2694
01:59:09.230 --> 01:59:14.060
and chia seeds and blueberries or oatmeal.

2695
01:59:14.060 --> 01:59:17.540
And then lunches, maybe some sort of burrito

2696
01:59:17.540 --> 01:59:19.660
or it might be something that...

2697
01:59:21.720 --> 01:59:22.820
I don't eat a lot of salads,

2698
01:59:22.820 --> 01:59:25.398
but I might eat a salad a couple of days a week.

2699
01:59:25.398 --> 01:59:28.610
Do you make that, and then put it in the backpack?

2700
01:59:28.610 --> 01:59:29.443
Yeah, I do.

2701
01:59:29.443 --> 01:59:31.470
I like to put that tuff in my backpack,

2702
01:59:31.470 --> 01:59:32.420
sometimes my backpack is full.

2703
01:59:32.420 --> 01:59:34.520
So you're not eating in the school's cafeteria, lunch.

2704
01:59:34.520 --> 01:59:35.560
Not too frequently.

2705
01:59:35.560 --> 01:59:37.810
Unfortunately like once in a blue moon,

2706
01:59:37.810 --> 01:59:39.210
they might have something I can eat,

2707
01:59:39.210 --> 01:59:41.420
but they don't have a lot of offerings.

2708
01:59:41.420 --> 01:59:44.623
Like our school program, we still have to work on it.

2709
01:59:46.256 --> 01:59:47.730
But I like ethnic foods.

2710
01:59:47.730 --> 01:59:51.490
So like Thai food, Indian, Mexican food,

2711
01:59:51.490 --> 01:59:54.810
all those sayings, in Circleville is more challenging.

2712
01:59:54.810 --> 01:59:56.610
Like that's a rural community.

2713
01:59:56.610 --> 02:00:00.460
So we have in circle builders, literally only Chipotle

2714
02:00:00.460 --> 02:00:02.710
and like a Mexican restaurant we eat at.

2715
02:00:02.710 --> 02:00:04.850
And then otherwise I'd have to buy stuff

2716
02:00:04.850 --> 02:00:07.423
at our grocery store and throw it together.

2717
02:00:09.010 --> 02:00:10.990
But do you like the cooking part

2718
02:00:10.990 --> 02:00:13.510
or are you just on the move?

2719
02:00:13.510 --> 02:00:14.343
I'm on the move.

2720
02:00:14.343 --> 02:00:15.570
So I'm so bad.

2721
02:00:15.570 --> 02:00:19.260
I wouldn't mind cooking, but I rarely cook.

2722
02:00:19.260 --> 02:00:23.640
Like I literally just go and buy things fast in Cincinnati,

2723
02:00:23.640 --> 02:00:24.483
out to eat,

2724
02:00:25.520 --> 02:00:29.080
or my mom, I take her out to eat

2725
02:00:29.080 --> 02:00:31.190
a couple of nights a week in Cincinnati.

2726
02:00:31.190 --> 02:00:35.920
So we go out to eat but I don't make food that often.

2727
02:00:35.920 --> 02:00:37.290
Kelly's really gotten into it

2728
02:00:37.290 --> 02:00:40.163
and I just get to be the free rider on that.

2729
02:00:42.159 --> 02:00:44.880
It would be a great idea as I'm definitely like...

2730
02:00:44.880 --> 02:00:49.008
I'm interested in idea but I don't dedicate the time to it,

2731
02:00:49.008 --> 02:00:50.410
I'm just always on the move.

2732
02:00:50.410 --> 02:00:52.760
No, I get it, man, I get it.

2733
02:00:52.760 --> 02:00:55.730
The last thing I wanna explore with you,

2734
02:00:55.730 --> 02:00:57.480
we've been going for two hours now,

2735
02:00:58.470 --> 02:01:03.470
is I imagine somebody who's listening to this

2736
02:01:04.146 --> 02:01:04.979
and they're like,

2737
02:01:04.979 --> 02:01:07.637
"Man, I can't believe this guy does all this stuff.

2738
02:01:07.637 --> 02:01:08.777
"How does he do it?

2739
02:01:08.777 --> 02:01:09.803
"I'm inspired.

2740
02:01:10.797 --> 02:01:13.697
"It's time I got to get off the couch

2741
02:01:13.697 --> 02:01:17.610
"and rectify this couch potato situation I'm in."

2742
02:01:17.610 --> 02:01:21.440
How do you catalyze that spark,

2743
02:01:21.440 --> 02:01:22.560
get people off the couch,

2744
02:01:22.560 --> 02:01:25.763
get them active, how does somebody begin that journey?

2745
02:01:27.012 --> 02:01:29.130
I think the most important thing,

2746
02:01:29.130 --> 02:01:34.130
is to find something that you love or you have fun with.

2747
02:01:34.670 --> 02:01:36.950
And I know it doesn't become a love immediately.

2748
02:01:36.950 --> 02:01:38.820
I've heard people like they start running

2749
02:01:38.820 --> 02:01:41.420
and it takes three months before they actually start

2750
02:01:41.420 --> 02:01:42.550
to like it a little bit.

2751
02:01:42.550 --> 02:01:43.383
I get it.

2752
02:01:43.383 --> 02:01:45.620
They have that period where is, "I don't like this."

2753
02:01:46.610 --> 02:01:48.680
Getting in running shape is the worst.

2754
02:01:48.680 --> 02:01:51.330
Yeah, find something you enjoy,

2755
02:01:51.330 --> 02:01:52.650
it might be hiking,

2756
02:01:52.650 --> 02:01:54.540
it might be like swimming or whatever it is.

2757
02:01:54.540 --> 02:01:56.980
But finding something you have fun with,

2758
02:01:56.980 --> 02:01:59.720
also having accountability partners.

2759
02:01:59.720 --> 02:02:02.610
So with Cincinnati is a really nice city,

2760
02:02:02.610 --> 02:02:03.800
because we have actually

2761
02:02:03.800 --> 02:02:05.890
like eight different running groups.

2762
02:02:05.890 --> 02:02:08.250
You can join, they're just...

2763
02:02:09.175 --> 02:02:10.810
That's not even counting the private running groups

2764
02:02:10.810 --> 02:02:12.113
everyone is part of.

2765
02:02:13.489 --> 02:02:15.490
And joining a program,

2766
02:02:15.490 --> 02:02:18.270
it can be really helpful to have that motivation,

2767
02:02:18.270 --> 02:02:20.700
like, okay, every Wednesday or every Saturday,

2768
02:02:20.700 --> 02:02:22.260
we're gonna meet up and run.

2769
02:02:22.260 --> 02:02:26.490
I think having some sort of accountability partner is huge.

2770
02:02:26.490 --> 02:02:29.060
Having goals, like having a goal out there.

2771
02:02:29.060 --> 02:02:32.030
I like to make my list of like 10 to 12 goals.

2772
02:02:32.030 --> 02:02:35.270
Like I do it over the winter break.

2773
02:02:35.270 --> 02:02:37.430
Like I do that before January 1st comes.

2774
02:02:37.430 --> 02:02:39.620
It might sound kind of like...

2775
02:02:39.620 --> 02:02:41.700
But literally that's powerful to me,

2776
02:02:41.700 --> 02:02:44.100
because it sets out, where I wanna go for the year.

2777
02:02:44.100 --> 02:02:46.950
And I like to dream big and go for big things.

2778
02:02:46.950 --> 02:02:49.110
But having like goals out there,

2779
02:02:49.110 --> 02:02:52.330
something that you can like sign up for three months out,

2780
02:02:52.330 --> 02:02:54.320
that's huge.

2781
02:02:54.320 --> 02:02:55.440
Keeping things interesting.

2782
02:02:55.440 --> 02:02:57.990
So I have routines, I do certain routines,

2783
02:02:57.990 --> 02:03:00.700
like my run to work is pretty routine,

2784
02:03:00.700 --> 02:03:03.330
but I also like to mix it up.

2785
02:03:03.330 --> 02:03:05.120
So on a Saturday or Sunday,

2786
02:03:05.120 --> 02:03:07.220
I wanna go explore something new.

2787
02:03:07.220 --> 02:03:08.500
So like in Ohio,

2788
02:03:08.500 --> 02:03:11.660
I've had this goal of going to all 88 counties

2789
02:03:11.660 --> 02:03:14.917
and it's probably take me another 20 years to get there.

2790
02:03:14.917 --> 02:03:16.517
It's like a Ricky gates thing.

2791
02:03:17.532 --> 02:03:18.780
And I go out to state parks.

2792
02:03:18.780 --> 02:03:20.550
I try to see a new trail,

2793
02:03:20.550 --> 02:03:23.440
but I also like to do that with countries.

2794
02:03:23.440 --> 02:03:26.280
So I've now met 101 countries

2795
02:03:26.280 --> 02:03:29.320
and I' like to go see a new country

2796
02:03:29.320 --> 02:03:32.220
and incorporate some adventure with that,

2797
02:03:32.220 --> 02:03:36.103
'cause it kind of sparks running as well with what I do.

2798
02:03:37.900 --> 02:03:41.050
But having a little thing that you do,

2799
02:03:41.050 --> 02:03:42.760
maybe once every couple of weeks

2800
02:03:42.760 --> 02:03:46.910
where you get off your normal routine, it can be powerful.

2801
02:03:46.910 --> 02:03:48.510
It doesn't have to go to another country

2802
02:03:48.510 --> 02:03:49.960
or even another county,

2803
02:03:49.960 --> 02:03:53.320
but just trying something a little bit different

2804
02:03:53.320 --> 02:03:54.670
with what you're doing,

2805
02:03:54.670 --> 02:03:58.000
It brings a nuance to it that makes it,

2806
02:03:58.000 --> 02:04:01.420
so that you'll have more longevity with it.

2807
02:04:01.420 --> 02:04:05.220
One of the daily habits that you've adopted

2808
02:04:05.220 --> 02:04:06.620
that I think is super interesting,

2809
02:04:06.620 --> 02:04:11.250
is this personal journaling that is beyond journaling.

2810
02:04:11.250 --> 02:04:13.623
Like you write down everything that you do.

2811
02:04:15.073 --> 02:04:15.906
Is that true?

2812
02:04:15.906 --> 02:04:16.739
Well, not everything,

2813
02:04:16.739 --> 02:04:18.090
but I should have bring my calendar in here.

2814
02:04:18.090 --> 02:04:20.787
I got this old school calendar

2815
02:04:20.787 --> 02:04:22.570
It's like every day I count everything that do.

2816
02:04:22.570 --> 02:04:23.793
Yeah, it's kind of funny,

2817
02:04:23.793 --> 02:04:25.550
most as occasionally they'll see my calendar

2818
02:04:25.550 --> 02:04:27.060
on my desk.

2819
02:04:27.060 --> 02:04:28.600
So what are you doing at that?

2820
02:04:28.600 --> 02:04:29.900
Don't you write down on your phone?

2821
02:04:29.900 --> 02:04:31.444
No, I'm like old school.

2822
02:04:31.444 --> 02:04:35.483
I've got all my runs in there, all my plots,

2823
02:04:36.900 --> 02:04:38.163
whatever is happening.

